# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  laz_role_features — WHO WAS FAVOURED BEFORE THE MATCH, AND ARE THEY LOSING? [v75_12]
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  THE FEATURE THE BLIND SEARCH COULD NOT INVENT. A threshold search combines whatever is
#  in the frame; it has no way to construct "was the side now trailing the one the market
#  favoured at kick-off". That axis is domain knowledge, and on football it splits the
#  population harder than anything the search found on its own:
#      all trailing sides            609,383 ticks    8.0% strike   odds 15.89   ROI  +26.7%
#      the FAVOURITE trailing         38,734          41.0%         odds 12.48   ROI +411.1%
#      favourite trailing, early      14,027          63.2%         odds  4.92   ROI +211.3%
#      favourite trailing, early
#        AND the goal just went in     7,861          67.5%         odds  5.01   ROI +238.0%
#  From 8.0% to 67.5% on one idea the search had no vocabulary for.
#
#  WHY IT WORKS, AND WHY IT SHOULD GENERALISE. A pre-match favourite is the market's own
#  estimate of which side is stronger over a full match. When that side falls behind, the
#  live price reprices on the SCOREBOARD, which is one event, while the underlying edge
#  that produced the favourite tag is still there for the remaining time. The earlier the
#  deficit, the more time that edge has to express itself -- which is why prog < 0.35
#  outperforms prog < 0.5, and why a deficit that JUST opened (cb_prog < 0.1) is the
#  sharpest cut: the market has moved but the match has not yet.
#
#  CAUSAL BY CONSTRUCTION. pm_fav is latched from the FIRST tick of the match and never
#  updated, so nothing here reads forward. It is the same discipline as the tennis dog
#  role, which had to be fixed pre-match rather than taken per-tick -- taking the max at
#  every tick switched sides exactly when an underdog was winning.
#
#  EMITTED FOR EVERY SPORT. The read is not football-specific: a pre-match favourite behind
#  on the scoreboard is the same situation in basketball, tennis and both e-sports. The
#  deficit is goals, points or sets depending on the sport, and prog is already normalised
#  per sport, so the same four features carry across.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  FOOTBALL MARKET VOCABULARY — columns the feed carries and nothing has used   [v75_26]
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  --undeclared football listed 21 columns that could not be searched because nobody had
#  said when they become readable. Several are not incidental: they are the market's own
#  live opinion, published every tick, on 1.69-1.75 million rows.
#
#  Each entry below states the class, and the class is the CLAIM the causality gate
#  enforces. Ranges are measured on AB_football.parquet, not assumed:
#
#    prematch_draw_odds  1,749,679 rows   1.04 .. 51.00   median 3.50
#    draw_odds           1,734,924 rows   1.00 .. 151.0   median 3.65
#    req_rate            1,686,186 rows  -1.50 ..  8.00   median 0.047
#    total_goals_under   1,689,339 rows   1.00 .. 81.00   median 1.70
#    total_goals_handicap 1,689,339 rows  0.50 .. 17.50   median 2.50
#    u_open              1,744,818 rows   1.00 .. 26.00   median 1.75
#    leader_odds_rt        966,877 rows   1.00 .. 80.00   median 1.12
#    leading_team_rt     TEXT: Home_team | Away_team | Draw
#
#  WHY THESE MATTER. The draw pair is the piece football has that basketball does not: a
#  three-way market where the middle outcome carries information neither side does. A draw
#  price that has NOT moved while the score has is the book declining to update, and that
#  is the same shape as the mispricings the tennis book exploits through pm_ratio.
#
#  leading_team_rt is TEXT and cannot be searched as a threshold, so it is turned into the
#  numeric fb_lead_side and the derived fb_leader_is_fav -- the live favourite-role read
#  that pairs with the pre-match one in laz_role_features.
#
#  ALL ARE 'row' OR 'anchor'. Nothing here needs the match to finish, so all are portable
#  and legal at every tick. u_open and prematch_draw_odds are anchors -- fixed before play
#  or latched at first sight; the rest are quoted live on the tick they appear on.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

def _laz_declare_football_market(verbose=False):
    """Declare the football market columns so the search can reach them."""
    D = laz_declare_feature
    _d = [
     ('prematch_draw_odds', 'anchor',
      'the DRAW price quoted before kick-off, fixed for the whole match. Low values mean '
      'the book expected a tight game; high values mean it expected one side to win. It is '
      'the market\'s own pre-match read on how separable the two teams are, and it is the '
      'natural denominator for judging how far the live draw price has travelled.',
      'the draw quote on the first observed tick, constant thereafter'),
     ('draw_odds', 'row',
      'the LIVE draw price at this tick. Football is a three-way market and the middle '
      'outcome carries information neither side does: a draw price that has not moved while '
      'the score has is the book declining to update, which is the same shape as the '
      'mispricing the tennis book exploits through pm_ratio.',
      'the draw quote on this tick'),
     ('req_rate', 'row',
      'goals still required per unit of remaining match, for the total-goals line in play. '
      'Near zero the line is already effectively settled; high values mean the game must '
      'accelerate sharply for the over to land. Median 0.047 on this feed, so the typical '
      'tick sits close to settled.',
      '(line - goals so far) / remaining share of the match'),
     ('total_goals_under', 'row',
      'the live UNDER price on the total-goals line quoted at this tick. Median 1.70, so '
      'the under is usually the favoured side of the totals book -- which is why an under '
      'quote drifting OUT while the game stays quiet is the anomaly worth reading.',
      'the under quote on this tick'),
     ('total_goals_handicap', 'row',
      'the total-goals LINE the under and over prices are quoted against at this tick. '
      'Median 2.50. Meaningless without the prices beside it, and the prices are '
      'meaningless without it -- a 1.70 under on a 1.5 line and on a 3.5 line are opposite '
      'bets.',
      'the goals line in force on this tick'),
     ('u_open', 'anchor',
      'the UNDER price at the open, latched at the first observed tick. The reference the '
      'live under price is judged against: u_odds / u_open above 1 means the book has '
      'lengthened the under since we first saw the match.',
      'the under quote on the first observed tick, constant thereafter'),
     ('leader_odds_rt', 'row',
      'the live moneyline price of whichever side is CURRENTLY ahead. Median 1.12, so the '
      'leader is usually short -- which makes the tail, a leader still priced long, the '
      'population where the market and the scoreboard disagree.',
      'home_odds or away_odds, selected by who leads on this tick'),
    ]
    for nm, sem, defn, formula in _d:
        try:
            D(nm, sem, defn, formula=formula, sport='football', verbose=verbose)
        except Exception as e:
            if verbose:
                print(f'[declare] {nm}: {type(e).__name__}: {str(e)[:60]}')


def laz_football_market_features(df, verbose=True):
    """Numeric features derived from the football market columns declared above."""
    try:
        mid = df['match_id']
        cols = {}
        _dr = pd.to_numeric(df.get('draw_odds'), errors='coerce')
        _pdr = pd.to_numeric(df.get('prematch_draw_odds'), errors='coerce')
        _u = pd.to_numeric(df.get('total_goals_under'), errors='coerce')
        _u0 = pd.to_numeric(df.get('u_open'), errors='coerce')
        _lo = pd.to_numeric(df.get('leader_odds_rt'), errors='coerce')
        _ho = pd.to_numeric(df.get('home_odds'), errors='coerce')
        _ao = pd.to_numeric(df.get('away_odds'), errors='coerce')

        # HOW FAR THE DRAW HAS TRAVELLED. Above 1 the draw has lengthened since kick-off --
        # the book now thinks a decisive result is likelier than it did. Below 1 it has
        # shortened, which after a goal means the book expects a reply.
        if _dr is not None and _pdr is not None:
            cols['fb_draw_rel'] = _dr / _pdr.replace(0, np.nan)
            cols['fb_draw_move'] = _dr - _pdr
        # THE UNDER AGAINST ITS OWN OPEN, the same read for the totals book.
        if _u is not None and _u0 is not None:
            cols['fb_u_rel'] = _u / _u0.replace(0, np.nan)
        # WHO LEADS, as a number. leading_team_rt is TEXT and cannot be a threshold.
        if 'leading_team_rt' in df.columns:
            _s = df['leading_team_rt'].astype(str).str.strip().str.lower()
            cols['fb_lead_side'] = np.where(_s.str.startswith('home'), 1.0,
                                            np.where(_s.str.startswith('away'), -1.0, 0.0))
            # Is the side currently ahead the one the market favoured at first sight? This
            # is the LIVE counterpart of laz_fav_trailing and pairs with it directly.
            _fh = pd.to_numeric(df.get('laz_pm_fav_home'), errors='coerce')
            if _fh is not None and not _fh.isna().all():
                _ls = pd.Series(cols['fb_lead_side'], index=df.index)
                cols['fb_leader_is_fav'] = np.where(
                    _ls == 0, 0.0,
                    ((_ls > 0) == (_fh > 0)).astype(float))
        # THE LEADER'S PRICE AGAINST THE BOOK'S OWN SHORTEST QUOTE. Above 1 means the side
        # in front is priced LONGER than the pre-match favourite was -- the scoreboard and
        # the market disagree, which is the population the comeback reads live in.
        if _lo is not None and _ho is not None and _ao is not None:
            _fav0 = np.minimum(mid.map(_ho.groupby(mid).first()),
                               mid.map(_ao.groupby(mid).first()))
            cols['fb_leader_vs_fav0'] = _lo / _fav0.replace(0, np.nan)
        added = 0
        for k, v in cols.items():
            if k not in df.columns:
                df[k] = pd.to_numeric(v, errors='coerce')
                added += 1
        if verbose and added:
            print(f'[fb-market] {added} feature(s) from the football market columns')
    except Exception as _fe2:
        if verbose:
            print(f'[fb-market] skipped ({type(_fe2).__name__}: {str(_fe2)[:60]})')
    return df


def laz_role_features(df, sport, verbose=True):
    """Pre-match favourite role, and whether that side is currently behind."""
    try:
        mid = df['match_id']
        ho = pd.to_numeric(df.get('home_odds'), errors='coerce')
        ao = pd.to_numeric(df.get('away_odds'), errors='coerce')
        if ho is None or ao is None or ho.isna().all():
            return df
        # Latched at the first tick that has both prices. Never recomputed.
        _f = pd.DataFrame({'m': mid, 'h': ho, 'a': ao}).dropna(subset=['h', 'a'])
        _first = _f.groupby('m')[['h', 'a']].first()
        h0 = mid.map(_first['h'])
        a0 = mid.map(_first['a'])
        fav_home = (h0 < a0)
        df['laz_pm_fav_home'] = fav_home.astype('float64')
        df['laz_pm_fav_odds'] = np.minimum(h0, a0)
        df['laz_pm_gap'] = (h0 - a0).abs()

        hs = pd.to_numeric(df.get('h_score'), errors='coerce')
        as_ = pd.to_numeric(df.get('a_score'), errors='coerce')
        sd = hs - as_
        trail_home = (sd < 0)
        trail_away = (sd > 0)
        # 1.0 when the side the market favoured BEFORE the match is the side now behind.
        df['laz_fav_trailing'] = np.where(
            sd == 0, 0.0,
            np.where((trail_home & fav_home) | (trail_away & ~fav_home), 1.0, 0.0))
        df['laz_fav_deficit'] = np.where(df['laz_fav_trailing'] > 0, sd.abs(), np.nan)
        # The favourite's own live price while it is behind -- what you would be backing.
        df['laz_fav_live_odds'] = np.where(
            df['laz_fav_trailing'] > 0, np.where(fav_home, ho, ao), np.nan)
        # How far the favourite's price has drifted OUT since kick-off. Above 1 means the
        # market has moved against it; that drift is the mispricing this exploits.
        _fav0 = np.where(fav_home, h0, a0)
        df['laz_fav_drift'] = np.where(
            df['laz_fav_trailing'] > 0,
            np.where(fav_home, ho, ao) / np.where(_fav0 > 0, _fav0, np.nan), np.nan)
        # ── OBSERVABILITY: HOW CLEAN IS THE ANCHOR? ──────────────────────────────────
        # The anchor is the FIRST TICK WITH VALID PRICES, not kick-off. That is correct and
        # deliberate: the feed is a scrape of a third-party API, and a match enters it when
        # that API publishes it, not when the referee starts the clock. First-observed IS
        # our first observation, so it is the honest anchor -- there is no earlier price to
        # have used.
        #
        # BUT IT IS NOT ALWAYS A CLEAN ONE, and the strategy has to know which it has.
        # Measured on 1,266 football matches:
        #     first observation at prog <= 0.05 (near kick-off)   750   59.2%
        #     first observation at prog >  0.25 (well underway)    59    4.7%
        #     A GOAL ALREADY SCORED at first observation          114    9.0%
        # In that last 9%, "the favourite" is not the market's pre-match view at all -- the
        # price already reflects the scoreline. A side priced short while 1-0 up is not the
        # same object as a side priced short before anyone scored, and treating them as one
        # mixes two populations.
        #
        # So the role is published WITH its provenance, and a strategy can require a clean
        # anchor (laz_anchor_clean >= 1) or accept a contaminated one deliberately.
        _obs_ok = ho.notna() & ao.notna()
        _first_idx = _obs_ok.groupby(mid, observed=True).transform('idxmax').where(_obs_ok.groupby(mid, observed=True).transform('any'))
        _pr_col = next((c for c in ('fb_prog', 'prog', 'prog_score') if c in df.columns), None)
        if _pr_col is not None:
            _pr = pd.to_numeric(df[_pr_col], errors='coerce')
            df['laz_anchor_prog'] = _pr.where(_obs_ok).groupby(mid).transform('first')
        else:
            df['laz_anchor_prog'] = np.nan
        _sd0 = sd.where(_obs_ok).groupby(mid).transform('first')
        df['laz_anchor_sd'] = _sd0
        # 1.0 when the scoreline was LEVEL at our first sight of the match, so the favourite
        # tag is the market's view of the teams rather than of the scoreboard.
        df['laz_anchor_clean'] = (_sd0.fillna(0) == 0).astype('float64')
        if verbose:
            _n = int((df['laz_fav_trailing'] > 0).sum())
            _cl = df.groupby(mid)['laz_anchor_clean'].first()
            print(f'[roles] {sport}: 9 role feature(s) added | {_n:,} ticks with the '
                  f'FAVOURITE behind | anchor level at first sight in '
                  f'{100*_cl.mean():.1f}% of matches')
    except Exception as _re3:
        if verbose:
            print(f'[roles] {sport}: skipped ({type(_re3).__name__}: {str(_re3)[:60]})')
    return df



# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  laz_checkpoint_features — STATE AT FIXED POINTS OF THE MATCH                 [v75_17]
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  THE HYPOTHESIS CLASS THE SEARCH CANNOT REACH. A threshold search reads the CURRENT tick.
#  It has no way to ask "was the favourite ahead at the halfway point of Q1, and what did
#  that mean for the finish?" -- that is a question about a REMEMBERED STATE, and the frame
#  carries no column for it.
#
#  Yet it is the natural shape of a betting read. A favourite level at the quarter mark is
#  a different object from a favourite two scores up; an underdog leading at half-time has
#  told you something the pre-match price did not. These are the questions a person asks,
#  and until now the engine could not represent them.
#
#  WHAT IS LATCHED. At each checkpoint -- 25%, 50%, 75% and 100% of match progress -- the
#  state is frozen and carried forward:
#      laz_cp{N}_sd        the score difference at that point, home-signed
#      laz_cp{N}_lead      who led: 1 home, -1 away, 0 level
#      laz_cp{N}_fav_lead  1.0 if the side we anchored as favourite was the one ahead
#      laz_cp{N}_swing     how much the score difference has moved SINCE that point
#  Four checkpoints x four values = 16 features, on every sport, from the same normalised
#  progress axis that already carries tennis sets, football halves and basketball quarters.
#
#  CAUSALITY IS THE WHOLE DESIGN. A checkpoint value does not exist until the match has
#  passed it. Each is registered in BB66_RESOLVES_AT against its own progress fraction, so
#  a stack entering at prog <= 0.30 CANNOT read laz_cp50_*, and the gate rejects it while
#  the candidate is being built rather than after it has been reported. This is the exact
#  failure that produced BBT03/04/06 -- a Q1 entry reading Q1's final margin -- and the
#  checkpoint family is where that mistake would otherwise be easiest to repeat.
#
#  BEFORE the checkpoint the value is NaN, so a condition on it simply does not fire. That
#  is deliberate: a strategy conditioning on the 50% state is, by construction, a
#  second-half strategy.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

LAZ_CHECKPOINTS = (0.25, 0.50, 0.75)



# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  laz_admit_feature — THE ONE DOOR A NEW FEATURE MUST PASS THROUGH             [v75_18]
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  A loop that invents features around the clock will invent broken ones. Every failure
#  mode below happened in a single working session, to features written carefully by hand:
#
#    NOT CLASSIFIED    fb_prog, laz_fav_trailing, seg_wins and lead_net were all searchable
#                      before anyone had said when they become readable. The deny-list gate
#                      passed every name it had not been told about.
#    NOT COMPUTED      the vectorised block raised AttributeError on a scalar and took the
#                      WHOLE basketball feature set down with it -- 33 features to 1, all 40
#                      strategies to zero -- while reporting nothing worse than a log line.
#    NOT LIVE          pm_dog_odds, ten_minute, dog_anchor, ten_dog_ml and ten_pm_ratio
#                      existed only in the backtest frame. TNQ02-05 would have fired in the
#                      book and NEVER in production.
#    NOT EQUAL         dog_anchor was implemented as a clipped distance where the backtest
#                      uses an unbounded ratio (divergence 5.43), and then with a per-tick
#                      role that SWITCHES SIDES when prices cross -- exactly when an
#                      underdog is winning (divergence 1.58).
#    NOT THE SAME NAME ten_pm_ratio was aliased from the OLD max/min pm_ratio while the
#                      tennis block rebuilt a role-relative one. Divergence 12.42 across
#                      75,620 rows. TNQ04/05 fell from 74% to 49-53%: same conditions,
#                      different feature. No gate caught it; reproducing the registered
#                      numbers against raw data did.
#
#  So admission is not advisory. A feature that has not passed every check below is not in
#  the search vocabulary, and a strategy cannot be built on it.
#
#  THE FIVE CHECKS
#    1  CLASSIFIED   window_semantics declares when it is readable: row | observed |
#                    anchor, or a phase in BB66_RESOLVES_AT. No classification, no entry.
#    2  COMPUTED     it actually appears in the augmented frame, with real values on a
#                    meaningful share of rows -- not a column of NaN.
#    3  SANE         finite, not constant, and inside any declared bounds. A constant
#                    column selects everything or nothing and will look like a strategy.
#    4  LIVE         the sport's live engine emits it. Backtest-only means production-never.
#    5  EQUAL        live value == backtest value to 1e-6, tick for tick, on real matches --
#                    AND on the same rows: a divergence in row COUNT is a divergence too,
#                    which is how ten_pm_ratio hid.
#
#  Returns (admitted, report). The report names the first failing check and why, so a loop
#  can act on it: an unclassified feature needs a semantics declaration, a non-live one
#  needs porting into the engine class, an unequal one needs its formula reconciled. Each
#  failure says what to fix, not merely that something is wrong.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  laz_ledger — THE PERMANENT HYPOTHESIS RECORD                                 [v75_19]
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  The engine has no memory between runs. Every session starts blank, which means the loop
#  can propose the same idea forever and never know it has already been tried.
#
#  Worse: it can congratulate itself. In one session the favourite-trailing hypothesis was
#  proposed, measured at 67.5% strike and +238% ROI, and only later found to read 11.8%
#  once the feature was built correctly -- the first number was an artefact of latching the
#  role from an already-filtered frame. BOTH numbers belong in the record. A ledger that
#  keeps only the survivors is a ledger that teaches the loop to trust its own mistakes.
#
#  FOUR RULES, EACH EARNED
#
#  1. PROPOSALS ARE CODE, NEVER NUMBERS. `hypothesis` and `feature_code` are what an LLM
#     writes. It does not write `measured`. Five fabricated results appeared in a single
#     session -- 100% strike on 332 bets from a circular settlement, +46.3% OOS on a
#     strategy whose own verification recorded +0.14%, three basketball stacks reading Q1's
#     result before it existed. A proposer that also reports produces plausible fiction at
#     machine speed.
#
#  2. THE FOUR GATES ARE THE BOUNDARY. causality · replay · live-computability · null
#     separation. `measured` may only be written by the engine, and `verdict` may only be
#     ADMITTED once all four are recorded as passed.
#
#  3. COMPUTE HONESTY. `restarts` and `pool_size` are mandatory on any search result. A
#     hypothesis is UNTESTED until restarts are in the same order as the search space:
#     14 restarts against 486 candidates is a null result ABOUT THE SEARCH, not about the
#     market. Such rows are recorded as UNDER_SEARCHED, never as REJECTED, so the loop
#     knows to return with more compute rather than crossing the idea off.
#
#  4. FAILURES CARRY THEIR NEXT STEP. `unlocks` is required on every non-admitted row.
#     "Didn't work" is a dead end; "the universe filter deleted the market, so retry every
#     market with per-market bands" is a direction. That distinction is what makes the loop
#     move forward instead of circling.
#
#  Stored as JSON Lines beside the checkpoints: append-only, one row per event, readable
#  by a human and by the next session's proposer.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Beside the checkpoints, in the same working directory the engine already owns.
LAZ_LEDGER_PATH = LAZ('laz_hypothesis_ledger.jsonl')

LAZ_VERDICTS = {
    'PROPOSED',        # written by the proposer; no measurement yet
    'ADMITTED',        # feature passed all five admission checks
    'MEASURED',        # search ran; see measured + restarts + pool_size
    'UNDER_SEARCHED',  # ran, but restarts << pool size: says nothing about the market
    'REJECTED',        # measured at adequate compute and did not clear the bar
    'FABRICATED',      # a number was recorded and later shown to be an artefact
    'REGISTERED',      # cleared the bar, passed the gates, written to laz_registry
}


def laz_ledger_append(kind, name, sport=None, hypothesis=None, feature_code=None,
                      conditions=None, verdict='PROPOSED', measured=None, gates=None,
                      restarts=None, pool_size=None, unlocks=None, stars=None,
                      supersedes=None, verbose=True):
    """Append one event. Refuses rows that would let the loop deceive itself."""
    import json, datetime
    if verdict not in LAZ_VERDICTS:
        raise ValueError(f'unknown verdict {verdict!r}; known: {sorted(LAZ_VERDICTS)}')

    # RULE 1 — a proposal may not carry numbers.
    if verdict == 'PROPOSED' and measured:
        raise ValueError('a PROPOSED row may not carry `measured`. Proposals are code and '
                         'reasoning; the engine writes the numbers. This separation is why '
                         'the loop cannot report its own performance.')

    # RULE 3 — a measurement without its compute is not a measurement.
    if verdict in ('MEASURED', 'REJECTED', 'REGISTERED') and measured is not None:
        if restarts is None or pool_size is None:
            raise ValueError('`restarts` and `pool_size` are mandatory on a measured row. '
                             'A search result without its compute cannot be told apart '
                             'from an under-powered one.')
        if verdict == 'REJECTED' and restarts < pool_size:
            verdict = 'UNDER_SEARCHED'
            unlocks = (unlocks or '') + (
                f' | RECLASSIFIED: {restarts} restarts against a pool of {pool_size} says '
                f'nothing about the market. Return with restarts >= pool_size before '
                f'crossing this off.')

    # RULE 2 — ADMITTED and REGISTERED require the gates on the record.
    if verdict in ('ADMITTED', 'REGISTERED'):
        need = {'causality', 'replay', 'live', 'null'}
        have = set((gates or {}).keys())
        if not need.issubset(have):
            raise ValueError(f'{verdict} requires all four gates recorded; missing '
                             f'{sorted(need - have)}. The gates are the boundary between a '
                             f'measurement and a fabrication.')
        bad = [k for k, v in (gates or {}).items() if v not in (True, 'PASS', 'pass')]
        if bad:
            raise ValueError(f'{verdict} refused: gate(s) {bad} did not pass.')

    # RULE 4 — a failure must say what it opens.
    if verdict in ('REJECTED', 'UNDER_SEARCHED', 'FABRICATED') and not unlocks:
        raise ValueError(f'a {verdict} row must set `unlocks`: what does this failure make '
                         f'worth trying next? "Did not work" is a dead end; "the average '
                         f'hid a segment, split on the structural axis" is a direction.')

    row = dict(ts=datetime.datetime.now().isoformat(timespec='seconds'),
               engine=ENGINE_VERSION, kind=kind, name=name, sport=sport,
               hypothesis=hypothesis, feature_code=feature_code,
               conditions=list(conditions) if conditions else None,
               verdict=verdict, measured=measured, gates=gates,
               restarts=restarts, pool_size=pool_size, unlocks=unlocks,
               stars=stars, supersedes=supersedes)
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(LAZ_LEDGER_PATH), exist_ok=True)
        with open(LAZ_LEDGER_PATH, 'a', encoding='utf-8') as fh:
            fh.write(json.dumps(row, default=str) + '\n')
    except OSError as e:
        print(f'[ledger] could not write: {e}')
        return row
    if verbose:
        _s = f' {stars}' if stars else ''
        print(f'[ledger] {verdict}{_s}  {kind}/{name}' + (f' ({sport})' if sport else ''))
    return row


def laz_ledger_read(sport=None, verdict=None, kind=None):
    """Every recorded event, newest last. This is what a proposer reads first."""
    import json
    rows = []
    try:
        with open(LAZ_LEDGER_PATH, encoding='utf-8') as fh:
            for ln in fh:
                ln = ln.strip()
                if not ln:
                    continue
                try:
                    r = json.loads(ln)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                if sport and r.get('sport') != sport:
                    continue
                if verdict and r.get('verdict') != verdict:
                    continue
                if kind and r.get('kind') != kind:
                    continue
                rows.append(r)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    return rows


def laz_ledger_brief(sport=None, verbose=True):
    """What has been tried, what it cost, and what the failures opened.

    This is the proposer's starting context: it exists so the loop does not re-propose an
    idea already measured, and so an UNDER_SEARCHED row is picked back up with more compute
    rather than treated as a settled negative.
    """
    import collections
    rows = laz_ledger_read(sport=sport)
    by = collections.Counter(r.get('verdict') for r in rows)
    if verbose:
        print('=' * 84)
        print(f'  HYPOTHESIS LEDGER{" — " + sport if sport else ""}   {len(rows)} event(s)')
        print('=' * 84)
        for k in ('REGISTERED', 'ADMITTED', 'MEASURED', 'UNDER_SEARCHED', 'REJECTED',
                  'FABRICATED', 'PROPOSED'):
            if by.get(k):
                print(f'  {k:16s}{by[k]}')
        _open = [r for r in rows if r.get('verdict') == 'UNDER_SEARCHED']
        if _open:
            print(f'\n  WORTH RETURNING TO — measured below their own search space:')
            for r in _open[-8:]:
                print(f"    {r.get('name')} ({r.get('sport')}): {r.get('restarts')} restarts "
                      f"vs pool {r.get('pool_size')}")
        _fab = [r for r in rows if r.get('verdict') == 'FABRICATED']
        if _fab:
            print(f'\n  KNOWN ARTEFACTS — do not re-propose without reading why:')
            for r in _fab[-6:]:
                print(f"    {r.get('name')}: {str(r.get('unlocks'))[:78]}")
        _unl = [r for r in rows if r.get('unlocks') and r.get('verdict') != 'REGISTERED']
        if _unl:
            print(f'\n  DIRECTIONS OPENED BY FAILURES:')
            for r in _unl[-8:]:
                print(f"    {r.get('name')}: {str(r.get('unlocks'))[:78]}")
    return dict(total=len(rows), by_verdict=dict(by), rows=rows)


def laz_ledger_merge(src_paths, verbose=True):
    """[v75_61] Merge other machines' hypothesis ledgers into this one.

    Three machines meant three separate memories: the MacBook's tennis loop could not
    learn from the Mac mini's basketball failures, and every UNDER_SEARCHED row existed
    only on the machine that wrote it. Rows are deduplicated on their exact content, so
    merging the same file twice -- or a file that already contains this ledger -- adds
    nothing. Copy another machine's file into the run directory as
    laz_hypothesis_ledger_<machine>.jsonl and the startup check merges it for you.
    """
    import json, os as _o
    have = set()
    try:
        with open(LAZ_LEDGER_PATH, encoding='utf-8') as fh:
            for ln in fh:
                if ln.strip():
                    have.add(ln.strip())
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    added = 0
    for p in ([src_paths] if isinstance(src_paths, str) else list(src_paths or [])):
        try:
            with open(p, encoding='utf-8') as fh:
                new = [ln.strip() for ln in fh
                       if ln.strip() and ln.strip() not in have]
        except OSError as e:
            if verbose:
                print(f'[ledger] merge skipped {p}: {e}')
            continue
        ok = []
        for ln in new:
            try:
                json.loads(ln)
                ok.append(ln)
            except ValueError:
                continue
        if ok:
            _o.makedirs(_o.path.dirname(LAZ_LEDGER_PATH), exist_ok=True)
            with open(LAZ_LEDGER_PATH, 'a', encoding='utf-8') as fh:
                for ln in ok:
                    fh.write(ln + '\n')
                    have.add(ln)
            added += len(ok)
        if verbose:
            print(f'[ledger] merged {len(ok)} new row(s) from {p}')
    return added



# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  laz_declare_feature — CLASSIFICATION IS PART OF CREATING A FEATURE           [v75_24]
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  A feature is not finished when it computes. It is finished when someone has stated WHEN
#  IT BECOMES READABLE, because that is what separates a value the book can use from a
#  value production can never produce.
#
#  Until now classification was a separate step, done afterwards, by whoever remembered.
#  Every feature added during one working session -- fb_prog, laz_fav_trailing, seg_wins,
#  lead_net, and the whole checkpoint family -- was searchable before anyone had said when
#  it exists. Nothing rejected them; the causality gate was a deny-list and passed any name
#  it had not been told about. A loop inventing features around the clock outruns that
#  within a day.
#
#  This makes the declaration the ACT OF CREATION. You cannot register a feature without
#  saying what class it is, and a feature that has not been declared cannot enter the
#  search vocabulary at all.
#
#    'row'       derived from THIS tick alone -- scores, prices, ratios of them
#    'observed'  running statistics over ticks ALREADY SEEN -- cummin, shift(k), velocities
#    'anchor'    latched at or before the first observed tick -- opening prices, the
#                favourite role, pre-match ratings joined at ingest
#    'phase'     resolves only when a named phase completes -- requires resolves_at
#
#  A 'phase' declaration without resolves_at is refused: that is the exact shape of the
#  three basketball stacks that entered at q <= 1 while reading Q1's final margin and were
#  reported at +58% to +71% ROI before anyone counted their live firings.
#
#  The docstring is required too, and it is not decoration. `dog_anchor` was implemented
#  twice against the wrong definition -- once as a clipped distance where the backtest uses
#  an unbounded ratio (divergence 5.43), once with a role taken per tick instead of latched
#  (divergence 1.58). A written formula is what a replay failure is checked against.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

LAZ_DECLARED_PATH = LAZ('laz_declared_features.json')


def laz_declare_feature(name, semantics, definition, formula=None, resolves_at=None,
                        sport=None, source_cols=None, verbose=True, _persist=True):
    """Register a new feature's causality. Required before it can be searched.

    Returns the lineage record it wrote. Raises if the declaration is incomplete -- an
    unclassified feature is not admitted, it is refused.
    """
    ws = str(semantics or '').strip().lower()
    if ws not in (LAZ_CAUSAL_SEMANTICS | {'phase'}):
        raise ValueError(
            f'{name}: semantics must be one of {sorted(LAZ_CAUSAL_SEMANTICS | {"phase"})}, '
            f'not {semantics!r}. This is the claim about when the value exists, and the '
            f'causality gate enforces it -- there is no default.')
    if ws == 'phase' and resolves_at is None:
        raise ValueError(
            f'{name}: semantics="phase" requires resolves_at -- the phase at which the '
            f'value becomes readable. Without it the gate cannot tell a legal entry from '
            f'one that reads a result before it exists, which is how three basketball '
            f'stacks reached a report at +58% to +71% ROI on values that did not yet exist.')
    if not definition or len(str(definition).strip()) < 20:
        raise ValueError(
            f'{name}: a definition of at least 20 characters is required. It is what a '
            f'replay divergence is checked against -- dog_anchor was implemented twice '
            f'against the wrong quantity because nobody had written down which one it was.')

    if ws == 'phase':
        BB66_RESOLVES_AT[name] = float(resolves_at)
    else:
        LAZ_WINDOW_SEMANTICS[name] = ws

    rec = dict(definition=str(definition), window_semantics=ws,
               formula=formula or str(definition),
               live_expr=formula or str(definition),
               backtest_expr=formula or str(definition),
               source_cols=list(source_cols) if source_cols else [],
               sport=sport, declared_by='laz_declare_feature', engine=ENGINE_VERSION)
    if ws == 'phase':
        rec['resolves_at'] = float(resolves_at)
    try:
        V67_FEATURE_LINEAGE_FULL[name] = rec
        _LAZ_LINEAGE_ALL[name] = rec
    except Exception:
        pass
    # ── [v75_61] DECLARATIONS SURVIVE RESTARTS ────────────────────────────────────────
    # Everything a declaration creates lived only in memory: one restart and every
    # admitted feature was an orphan — its builder gone from the frames, its stacks
    # failing causality with NO LINEAGE RECORD. Now each successful declaration is
    # persisted, and at import every saved record is REPLAYED through this very
    # function — one code path, no second reconstruction mechanism to drift.
    if _persist:
        try:
            import json as _dj
            try:
                with open(LAZ_DECLARED_PATH, encoding='utf-8') as _fh:
                    _all = _dj.load(_fh)
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                _all = {}
            _all[str(name)] = dict(semantics=str(semantics),
                                   definition=str(definition),
                                   formula=(str(formula) if formula else None),
                                   resolves_at=resolves_at, sport=sport,
                                   engine=ENGINE_VERSION)
            with open(LAZ_DECLARED_PATH, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as _fh:
                _dj.dump(_all, _fh, indent=1, default=str)
        except Exception:
            pass

    # Prove the declaration actually satisfies the gate rather than assuming it does.
    legal, phase, why = laz_feature_causal_class(name)
    if not legal and phase is None:
        raise ValueError(f'{name}: declared but still not causal-legal — {why}')
    try:
        laz_ledger_append('feature', name, sport,
                          hypothesis=str(definition)[:400],
                          feature_code=formula, verdict='PROPOSED', verbose=False)
    except Exception:
        pass
    if verbose:
        _w = f'phase>={resolves_at:g}' if ws == 'phase' else ws
        print(f'[declare] {name}: {_w} — {str(definition)[:64]}')
        print(f'[declare]   causality {"ok" if legal else f"phase-gated from {phase:g}"}. '
              f'Run laz_admit_feature to check it computes, is live and replays to 1e-6.')
    return rec


# Declared at import, once laz_declare_feature exists. A column classified only when
# someone remembers to call the declarer is a column the search cannot reach -- which is
# the state --undeclared reported for 21 football columns.
try:
    _laz_declare_football_market(verbose=False)
except Exception as _de:
    print(f'[declare] football market vocabulary not declared: {type(_de).__name__}')


# [v75_61] Restore every persisted declaration by replaying it through
# laz_declare_feature itself. Failures are skipped (a record from a newer engine
# with unknown semantics must not kill the import), and _persist=False prevents a
# rewrite loop.
_LAZ_DECLARED_RESTORED = False


def laz_restore_declared(verbose=True):
    """[v75_61] Replay persisted declarations. LAZY, not at import: mid-import,
    laz_declare_feature's own dependencies are not all defined yet and every
    replay fails silently — diagnosed the hard way. Called by the startup check
    and self-invoked on first frame touch, once per process."""
    global _LAZ_DECLARED_RESTORED
    if _LAZ_DECLARED_RESTORED:
        return 0
    _LAZ_DECLARED_RESTORED = True
    import json
    try:
        with open(LAZ_DECLARED_PATH, encoding='utf-8') as fh:
            saved = {k: v for k, v in json.load(fh).items()
                     if isinstance(v, dict)}
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return 0
    n = 0
    for nm, rc in saved.items():
        if nm in LAZ_WINDOW_SEMANTICS:
            continue
        try:
            laz_declare_feature(nm, rc.get('semantics'), rc.get('definition'),
                                formula=rc.get('formula'),
                                resolves_at=rc.get('resolves_at'),
                                sport=rc.get('sport'), verbose=False,
                                _persist=False)
            # The formula IS the builder; admission registered it in the original
            # process, restoration registers it here — same string, same compiler.
            if rc.get('formula'):
                LAZ_PROPOSED_BUILDERS.setdefault(nm, str(rc['formula']))
            n += 1
        except Exception:
            continue
    if n and verbose:
        print(f'[declare] {n} declared feature(s) restored from disk')
    return n


def laz_undeclared_features(sport=None, frame=None, verbose=True):
    """Every column the engine produces that has never been classified.

    These are the ones that cannot be searched. The list is the work queue: each needs a
    laz_declare_feature call stating when it becomes readable, and until it has one it
    cannot produce a strategy that fires in the book and never in production.
    """
    import os
    cols = set()
    if frame is not None:
        cols |= set(frame.columns)
    elif sport:
        p = os.path.join(FRAMES_DIR, f'AB_{sport}.parquet')
        if os.path.exists(p):
            try:
                cols |= set(pd.read_parquet(p).columns)
            except Exception:
                pass
    out = []
    for c in sorted(cols):
        legal, phase, why = laz_feature_causal_class(c)
        if not legal and phase is None and 'settlement' not in why:
            out.append((c, why))
    if verbose:
        print(f'[undeclared] {sport or "frame"}: {len(out)} column(s) cannot be searched')
        for c, why in out[:20]:
            print(f'    {c:28s}{why[:52]}')
        if out:
            print('  Declare each with laz_declare_feature(name, semantics, definition) '
                  'before it can enter a search.')
    return out



# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  PORTABILITY — "not yet in the live engine" is not "cannot work live"          [v75_25]
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  The live-computability gate refused any feature the sport's engine class did not already
#  emit. That conflates two entirely different states:
#
#      NOT PORTABLE   the value cannot exist at the entry tick at all -- it reads a result,
#                     a half-time aggregate, or a phase that has not completed. No amount
#                     of work makes this live. REJECT, permanently.
#      NOT YET PORTED the value is computable from what a live tick carries, but nobody has
#                     written it into the engine class yet. This is a task, not a defect.
#
#  Treating the second as the first throws away working strategies for a reason that is
#  purely about implementation order. TNQ02-05 were blocked this way and then unblocked
#  twenty minutes later by adding six lines to V67TenFeatureEngine -- the strategies were
#  never the problem.
#
#  PORTABILITY IS PROVED BY THE DECLARATION, NOT ASSUMED. The three tick-safe classes are
#  exactly the mechanically portable ones:
#      'row'       needs only this tick's fields          -> a pure function of the tick
#      'observed'  needs running state over ticks seen     -> the engine already keeps state
#                                                             per match; this is one more
#      'anchor'    needs a value latched at the first tick -> the engine already latches
#                                                             S.ph / S.pa the same way
#  A feature in any of those classes CAN be ported, by construction. One that is phase-gated
#  can also be ported, and simply returns None before its phase.
#
#  SO: a strategy reading an unported-but-portable feature is admitted as PENDING_LIVE_PORT.
#  It is registered, it appears in the book, its numbers are real -- and it CANNOT BE
#  STAKED until the port is written and replay matches to 1e-6 on the same rows. The
#  distinction is recorded on the strategy so nobody has to remember it.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

import inspect

LAZ_PORTABLE_SEMANTICS = {'row', 'observed', 'anchor', 'tick', 'live', 'prematch'}

# [v75_61] ONE PARSE PER ENGINE CLASS PER PROCESS. inspect.getsource re-reads and
# re-parses this entire multi-megabyte file on every call, and the book writer asks
# about every feature of every row -- so a 33-row book cost minutes of pure re-parsing
# (caught by a stack dump at the exact line). The source of an imported class cannot
# change within a process, so caching it is exact, not approximate. Verdicts are NOT
# cached: LAZ_WINDOW_SEMANTICS grows at runtime as features are declared, and a stale
# verdict would be a fabricated gate.
_LAZ_ENGINE_SRC_CACHE = {}


def laz_feature_portability(name, sport=None):
    """(state, why) — one of PORTED, PORTABLE, PHASE_PORTABLE, NOT_PORTABLE.

    PORTED and PORTABLE both allow a strategy to be registered; only PORTED allows it to be
    staked. NOT_PORTABLE is permanent: the value cannot exist at entry however it is coded.
    """
    legal, phase, why = laz_feature_causal_class(name)
    if not legal and phase is None:
        return 'NOT_PORTABLE', why
    base = name.split('_', 1)[1] if name.startswith(
        ('efb_', 'ebb_', 'ten_', 'fb_', 'bb_', 'laz_')) else name
    ws = (LAZ_WINDOW_SEMANTICS.get(name) or LAZ_WINDOW_SEMANTICS.get(base) or '')
    # Already emitted by the sport's live engine?
    eng = _LAZ_ENGINE_FOR_SPORT.get(sport) if sport else None
    if eng is not None:
        try:
            src = _LAZ_ENGINE_SRC_CACHE.get(eng)
            if src is None:
                src = inspect.getsource(eng)
                _LAZ_ENGINE_SRC_CACHE[eng] = src
            if f"'{name}'" in src or f'"{name}"' in src:
                return 'PORTED', 'emitted by the live engine'
        except Exception:
            pass
    if phase is not None:
        return 'PHASE_PORTABLE', (f'resolves at phase {phase:g}; portable as a latch that '
                                  f'returns None until then')
    if ws in LAZ_PORTABLE_SEMANTICS:
        _how = {'row': 'a pure function of the current tick',
                'observed': 'running state over ticks already seen -- the engine keeps '
                            'per-match state already',
                'anchor': 'a value latched at the first tick -- the engine latches S.ph / '
                          'S.pa the same way'}.get(ws, ws)
        return 'PORTABLE', f'window_semantics={ws}: {_how}'
    return 'NOT_PORTABLE', f'window_semantics={ws or "undeclared"} is not tick-safe'


def laz_strategy_port_state(conditions, sport, verbose=False):
    """Whether a stack can be registered, and whether it can be staked.

    Returns (registerable, stakeable, report). A stack is registerable when every feature
    is at least PORTABLE; stakeable only when every feature is PORTED and has replayed.
    """
    feats = sorted({str(c).split()[0] for c in (conditions or []) if isinstance(c, str)})
    states = {f: laz_feature_portability(f, sport) for f in feats}
    blocked = [f for f, (st, _) in states.items() if st == 'NOT_PORTABLE']
    pending = [f for f, (st, _) in states.items()
               if st in ('PORTABLE', 'PHASE_PORTABLE')]
    rep = dict(features=states, blocked=blocked, pending_port=pending)
    registerable = not blocked
    stakeable = registerable and not pending
    rep['port_status'] = ('BLOCKED_NOT_PORTABLE' if blocked else
                          'PENDING_LIVE_PORT' if pending else 'READY_SHADOW_FIRST')
    if pending and not blocked:
        rep['todo'] = (f'port {", ".join(pending)} into '
                       f'{getattr(_LAZ_ENGINE_FOR_SPORT.get(sport), "__name__", "the live engine")}'
                       f'.compute, then re-run laz_admit_feature so replay confirms 1e-6 on '
                       f'the same rows. Until then this strategy is registered and NOT '
                       f'stakeable.')
    if verbose:
        for f, (st, why) in states.items():
            print(f'  {f:26s}{st:16s}{why[:56]}')
        print(f'  -> {rep["port_status"]}'
              + (f'  |  {rep["todo"]}' if rep.get('todo') else ''))
    return registerable, stakeable, rep



# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  laz_build_proposed — MAKE THE PROPOSER'S FORMULA ACTUALLY COMPUTE            [v75_51]
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  The proposer writes a formula. Nothing evaluated it, so admission stopped at check 2 --
#  "not present in the augmented frame" -- and every proposal died there. The loop could
#  declare ideas and never test one, which meant the scorecard had nothing to learn from
#  and the LLM half of the engine was decoration on a search that works without it.
#
#  This evaluates the formula against the frame. It is deliberately NARROW:
#    * only column names that exist in the frame, numbers, and + - * / ( ) are allowed
#    * no attribute access, no calls, no imports, no names outside the frame
#  A formula referencing anything else is refused, not guessed at. That is not paranoia
#  about a local model -- it is the same discipline as the causality gate: a feature whose
#  inputs cannot be named cannot be reproduced in production either.
#
#  The result is registered as a column, so laz_admit_feature can then run checks 2-5 on it
#  and _laz_search_pool can put thresholds on it. That is the loop closing: propose ->
#  compute -> admit -> search -> score -> propose again, with the scorecard reporting which
#  shapes of idea this market actually rewards.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

import re as _laz_re

_LAZ_FORMULA_OK = _laz_re.compile(r'^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$')


# ── [v75_61] THE VERB SET — the proposer can finally say what has ever paid ──────────
#  The old formula language was names, numbers and + - * / ( ). That cannot express a
#  first-tick latch, a running statistic, or a velocity -- which is the ENTIRE class of
#  constructed feature that has ever cleared the bar in this project (laz_fav_trailing,
#  the checkpoint family). One of the engine's own four offline proposals used min() and
#  died at its own admission. So the proposer was structurally limited to arithmetic
#  blends the threshold search already approximates.
#
#  Ten verbs now exist. Each is COMPILED BY THE ENGINE into per-match pandas operations
#  on a (match_id, time)-sorted view -- never raw eval of model output -- and each is
#  causal by construction:
#      anchor(x)        x latched at the match's first valid tick        (class: anchor)
#      runmax(x)        expanding max over ticks already seen             (class: observed)
#      runmin(x)        expanding min                                     (class: observed)
#      runmean(x)       expanding mean                                    (class: observed)
#      prev(x, k)       x as it was k ticks ago in this match             (class: observed)
#      chg(x, k)        x - prev(x, k): velocity over k ticks             (class: observed)
#      ticks()          how many ticks this match has shown so far        (class: observed)
#      pairmin(a, b)    elementwise min of two expressions                (class: row)
#      pairmax(a, b)    elementwise max                                   (class: row)
#      mag(x)           absolute value                                    (class: row)
#  laz_fav_trailing -- "is the side now trailing the one the market favoured at first
#  sight" -- is now writable as (anchor(home_odds) - anchor(away_odds)) * (h_score -
#  a_score) > 0 after a threshold. The gates after this are unchanged: declare, admit,
#  search, null. The verbs widen what can be SAID, not what is believed.
LAZ_FORMULA_VERBS = ('anchor', 'runmax', 'runmin', 'runmean', 'prev', 'chg', 'ticks',
                     'pairmin', 'pairmax', 'mag')


def _laz_formula_series(df, expr, name='formula', verbose=True):
    """Compile a proposer formula (arithmetic + the verb set) into a Series, or None.

    Parsed with ast -- there is no eval of model-authored text anywhere. Allowed nodes:
    +, -, *, /, unary +/-, numeric literals, frame column names, and calls to the verbs
    above (prev/chg take an optional integer literal k, default 1). Anything else is
    refused with the exact reason, which the repair turn hands back to the model.
    Per-match verbs are computed on a (match_id, time)-sorted view and re-aligned to the
    frame's own index, so row order in the stored frame cannot corrupt causality.
    """
    import ast
    if not expr or not isinstance(expr, str):
        return None
    try:
        tree = ast.parse(expr.strip(), mode='eval')
    except SyntaxError as e:
        if verbose:
            print(f'[build] {name}: not a valid expression ({str(e)[:60]})')
        return None
    nodes = list(ast.walk(tree))
    if len(nodes) > 200:
        if verbose:
            print(f'[build] {name}: expression too complex ({len(nodes)} nodes, cap 200)')
        return None
    _cols, _bad, _verbs_used = set(), [], set()
    for nd in nodes:
        if isinstance(nd, (ast.Expression, ast.Load)):
            continue
        elif isinstance(nd, ast.BinOp) and isinstance(nd.op, (ast.Add, ast.Sub,
                                                              ast.Mult, ast.Div)):
            continue
        elif isinstance(nd, (ast.Add, ast.Sub, ast.Mult, ast.Div, ast.USub, ast.UAdd)):
            continue
        elif isinstance(nd, ast.UnaryOp) and isinstance(nd.op, (ast.USub, ast.UAdd)):
            continue
        elif isinstance(nd, ast.Constant) and isinstance(nd.value, (int, float)):
            continue
        elif isinstance(nd, ast.Call):
            if not (isinstance(nd.func, ast.Name) and nd.func.id in LAZ_FORMULA_VERBS):
                _bad.append(f'call to {getattr(nd.func, "id", "?")!r} — only these '
                            f'functions exist: {", ".join(LAZ_FORMULA_VERBS)}')
            elif nd.keywords:
                _bad.append(f'{nd.func.id}: keyword arguments are not supported')
            else:
                _verbs_used.add(nd.func.id)
            continue
        elif isinstance(nd, ast.Name):
            if nd.id not in LAZ_FORMULA_VERBS:
                _cols.add(nd.id)
            continue
        else:
            _bad.append(f'{type(nd).__name__} is not allowed — formulas are arithmetic '
                        f'over column names plus the verb set')
    missing = sorted(c for c in _cols if c not in df.columns)
    _fw = sorted(c for c in _cols if _laz_is_forward(c))
    if _fw:
        _bad.append(f'forward-looking column(s) {_fw[:4]} — settled results may '
                    f'never feed a condition; refused at the source, not merely '
                    f'gated later')
    if missing:
        _bad.append(f'unknown column(s) {missing[:5]} — a feature whose inputs cannot '
                    f'be named cannot be reproduced live either')
    if _bad:
        if verbose:
            print(f'[build] {name}: refused — {"; ".join(_bad[:3])[:220]}')
        return None
    # ── The per-match context: a (match_id, time)-sorted view of what the verbs need ──
    _mcol = 'match_id' if 'match_id' in df.columns else None
    _tcol = next((c for c in ('ts', 'mstart', 'epoch_ts') if c in df.columns), None)
    _need_ctx = bool(_verbs_used & {'anchor', 'runmax', 'runmin', 'runmean', 'prev',
                                    'chg', 'ticks'})
    if _need_ctx and _mcol is None:
        if verbose:
            print(f'[build] {name}: per-match verbs need a match_id column and this '
                  f'frame has none')
        return None
    if _need_ctx:
        _ordidx = (df.sort_values([_mcol] + ([_tcol] if _tcol else []),
                                  kind='mergesort').index)
    else:
        _ordidx = df.index
    _g = df[_mcol].reindex(_ordidx) if _mcol is not None else None

    def _col(nm):
        return pd.to_numeric(df[nm], errors='coerce').reindex(_ordidx)

    def _ev(nd):
        if isinstance(nd, ast.Expression):
            return _ev(nd.body)
        if isinstance(nd, ast.Constant):
            return float(nd.value)
        if isinstance(nd, ast.Name):
            return _col(nd.id)
        if isinstance(nd, ast.UnaryOp):
            v = _ev(nd.operand)
            return -v if isinstance(nd.op, ast.USub) else v
        if isinstance(nd, ast.BinOp):
            a, b = _ev(nd.left), _ev(nd.right)
            if isinstance(nd.op, ast.Add):
                return a + b
            if isinstance(nd.op, ast.Sub):
                return a - b
            if isinstance(nd.op, ast.Mult):
                return a * b
            # division: 0 and inf become NaN downstream
            return a / b
        if isinstance(nd, ast.Call):
            fn = nd.func.id
            if fn == 'ticks':
                return _g.groupby(_g, sort=False).cumcount().astype('float64')
            if fn in ('pairmin', 'pairmax'):
                if len(nd.args) != 2:
                    raise ValueError(f'{fn} takes exactly two arguments')
                a, b = _ev(nd.args[0]), _ev(nd.args[1])
                a = pd.Series(a, index=_ordidx) if not isinstance(a, pd.Series) else a
                b = pd.Series(b, index=_ordidx) if not isinstance(b, pd.Series) else b
                return pd.Series(np.minimum(a, b) if fn == 'pairmin'
                                 else np.maximum(a, b), index=_ordidx)
            if fn == 'mag':
                if len(nd.args) != 1:
                    raise ValueError('mag takes exactly one argument')
                v = _ev(nd.args[0])
                return v.abs() if isinstance(v, pd.Series) else abs(v)
            # per-match verbs
            if fn in ('prev', 'chg'):
                if len(nd.args) not in (1, 2):
                    raise ValueError(f'{fn} takes (x) or (x, k)')
                k = 1
                if len(nd.args) == 2:
                    if not (isinstance(nd.args[1], ast.Constant)
                            and isinstance(nd.args[1].value, int)
                            and 1 <= nd.args[1].value <= 50):
                        raise ValueError(f'{fn}: k must be an integer literal 1..50')
                    k = nd.args[1].value
            elif len(nd.args) != 1:
                raise ValueError(f'{fn} takes exactly one argument')
            x = _ev(nd.args[0])
            if not isinstance(x, pd.Series):
                x = pd.Series(float(x), index=_ordidx)
            gx = x.groupby(_g, sort=False)
            if fn == 'anchor':
                # [v75_61] CAUSAL LATCH. transform('first') broadcasts the first
                # non-NA value to EVERY row of the match — including rows BEFORE
                # that value existed, a lookahead whenever a column starts NaN.
                # Fixed: rows before the first valid tick are NaN; the latch
                # holds from the tick it was actually observed.
                _fv = gx.transform('first')
                _seen = x.notna().groupby(_g, sort=False).cumsum()
                return _fv.where(_seen >= 1)
            if fn == 'runmax':
                return gx.cummax()
            if fn == 'runmin':
                return gx.cummin()
            if fn == 'runmean':
                return gx.cumsum() / (gx.cumcount() + 1.0)
            if fn == 'prev':
                return gx.shift(k)
            if fn == 'chg':
                return x - gx.shift(k)
        raise ValueError(f'unsupported node {type(nd).__name__}')

    try:
        out = _ev(tree)
    except Exception as e:
        if verbose:
            print(f'[build] {name}: formula failed ({type(e).__name__}: {str(e)[:60]})')
        return None
    if not isinstance(out, pd.Series):
        if verbose:
            print(f'[build] {name}: constant — selects everything or nothing')
        return None
    return pd.to_numeric(out, errors='coerce').reindex(df.index)


LAZ_PORTED_VERBS_PATH = LAZ('laz_ported_verbs.json')
LAZ_PORTED_VERBS = {}
try:
    import json as _pvj
    with open(LAZ_PORTED_VERBS_PATH, encoding='utf-8') as _pvf:
        LAZ_PORTED_VERBS.update({k: v for k, v in _pvj.load(_pvf).items()
                                 if isinstance(v, dict)})
except (OSError, ValueError):
    pass


def _laz_ported_verbs_save():
    import json
    try:
        with open(LAZ_PORTED_VERBS_PATH, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as fh:
            json.dump(LAZ_PORTED_VERBS, fh, indent=1, default=str)
    except OSError:
        pass


class LazVerbStream:
    """[v75_61] THE STREAMING PORT of the verb formula language — the live half.

    Same grammar, same verbs, same NaN semantics as the batch compiler, evaluated
    ONE TICK AT A TIME with per-match state: anchor latches the first valid value
    seen, runmax/runmin/runmean carry running state, prev/chg keep a k-deep buffer,
    ticks counts. A live engine computes a verb feature with three lines:

        vs = LazVerbStream("(anchor(home_odds) - anchor(away_odds)) * (h_score - a_score)")
        ...
        value = vs.update(match_id, {'home_odds': 2.1, 'away_odds': 1.75, ...})

    laz_verb_port_verify replays this class against the batch computation on the
    real frame and requires agreement to 1e-6 on every row (NaN patterns included)
    before a feature is recorded as stream-ported. The class IS the reference live
    implementation; parity is proven, not assumed.
    """

    def __init__(self, formula):
        import ast
        self.formula = str(formula)
        self.tree = ast.parse(self.formula.strip(), mode='eval')
        nodes = list(ast.walk(self.tree))
        if len(nodes) > 200:
            raise ValueError('expression too complex (cap 200 nodes)')
        self.columns, self._nid = set(), {}
        for nd in nodes:
            if isinstance(nd, ast.Call):
                if not (isinstance(nd.func, ast.Name)
                        and nd.func.id in LAZ_FORMULA_VERBS):
                    raise ValueError(f'unknown function '
                                     f'{getattr(nd.func, "id", "?")!r}')
                if nd.keywords:
                    raise ValueError('keyword arguments are not supported')
                self._nid[id(nd)] = len(self._nid)
            elif isinstance(nd, ast.Name) and nd.id not in LAZ_FORMULA_VERBS:
                self.columns.add(nd.id)
            elif isinstance(nd, (ast.Expression, ast.Load, ast.BinOp, ast.UnaryOp,
                                 ast.Add, ast.Sub, ast.Mult, ast.Div, ast.USub,
                                 ast.UAdd)):
                continue
            elif isinstance(nd, ast.Constant) and isinstance(nd.value, (int, float)):
                continue
            elif isinstance(nd, ast.Call):
                continue
            elif not isinstance(nd, (ast.Name,)):
                raise ValueError(f'{type(nd).__name__} is not allowed')
        self._match = object()
        self._st = {}

    def _reset(self):
        import collections
        self._st = {i: dict(anchor=None, mx=None, mn=None, s=0.0, rows=0,
                            buf=collections.deque(maxlen=50), t=0)
                    for i in self._nid.values()}

    def update(self, match_id, row):
        """Feed the next tick of `match_id`; returns the formula's value (float)."""
        import ast, math
        if match_id != self._match:
            self._match = match_id
            self._reset()
        NAN = float('nan')

        def _isnan(v):
            try:
                return v != v
            except Exception:
                return True

        def _num(v):
            try:
                f = float(v)
                return f
            except (TypeError, ValueError):
                return NAN

        def _ev(nd):
            if isinstance(nd, ast.Expression):
                return _ev(nd.body)
            if isinstance(nd, ast.Constant):
                return float(nd.value)
            if isinstance(nd, ast.Name):
                return _num(row.get(nd.id, NAN))
            if isinstance(nd, ast.UnaryOp):
                v = _ev(nd.operand)
                return -v if isinstance(nd.op, ast.USub) else v
            if isinstance(nd, ast.BinOp):
                a, b = _ev(nd.left), _ev(nd.right)
                if _isnan(a) or _isnan(b):
                    if isinstance(nd.op, (ast.Add, ast.Sub, ast.Mult, ast.Div)):
                        return NAN
                if isinstance(nd.op, ast.Add):
                    return a + b
                if isinstance(nd.op, ast.Sub):
                    return a - b
                if isinstance(nd.op, ast.Mult):
                    return a * b
                if b == 0.0:                      # IEEE like pandas: 1/0=inf, 0/0=nan
                    return NAN if a == 0.0 else math.copysign(math.inf, a) \
                        * (1 if b == 0.0 else 1)
                return a / b
            # Calls
            st = self._st[self._nid[id(nd)]]
            fn = nd.func.id
            if fn == 'ticks':
                out = float(st['t'])
                st['t'] += 1
                return out
            if fn in ('pairmin', 'pairmax'):
                a, b = _ev(nd.args[0]), _ev(nd.args[1])
                if _isnan(a) or _isnan(b):
                    return NAN
                return min(a, b) if fn == 'pairmin' else max(a, b)
            if fn == 'mag':
                v = _ev(nd.args[0])
                return abs(v) if not _isnan(v) else NAN
            k = 1
            if fn in ('prev', 'chg') and len(nd.args) == 2:
                k = int(nd.args[1].value)
            x = _ev(nd.args[0])
            if fn == 'anchor':
                if st['anchor'] is None and not _isnan(x):
                    st['anchor'] = x
                return NAN if st['anchor'] is None else st['anchor']
            if fn == 'runmax':
                if not _isnan(x):
                    st['mx'] = x if st['mx'] is None else max(st['mx'], x)
                    return st['mx']
                return NAN
            if fn == 'runmin':
                if not _isnan(x):
                    st['mn'] = x if st['mn'] is None else min(st['mn'], x)
                    return st['mn']
                return NAN
            if fn == 'runmean':
                st['rows'] += 1
                if _isnan(x):
                    return NAN
                st['s'] += x
                return st['s'] / st['rows']
            if fn in ('prev', 'chg'):
                buf = st['buf']
                out = buf[-k] if len(buf) >= k else NAN
                buf.append(x)
                if fn == 'prev':
                    return out
                return NAN if (_isnan(x) or _isnan(out)) else x - out
            raise ValueError(fn)
        return _ev(self.tree)


def laz_verb_port_verify(name, formula, df, tol=1e-6, max_rows=250_000,
                         verbose=True):
    """[v75_61] Prove the streaming port equals the batch computation, row by row.

    The batch series (what every search measured) and LazVerbStream (what a live
    engine runs) are computed on the SAME (match_id, time)-sorted rows and must
    agree to `tol` everywhere, NaN patterns included — the identical standard the
    project has always demanded of a port (1e-6 on the same rows). On success the
    feature enters LAZ_PORTED_VERBS with its formula, row count and date, the
    replay gate turns PASS for stacks that use it, and the recorded formula is the
    exact string a live loop instantiates. Returns (ok, info).
    """
    import datetime as _dt
    batch = _laz_formula_series(df, formula, name=name, verbose=False)
    if batch is None:
        return False, 'formula does not compile on this frame'
    if 'match_id' not in df.columns:
        return False, 'no match_id column'
    tcol = next((c for c in ('ts', 'mstart', 'epoch_ts') if c in df.columns), None)
    order = df.sort_values(['match_id'] + ([tcol] if tcol else []),
                           kind='mergesort')
    if len(order) > max_rows:
        order = order.head(max_rows)
    try:
        vs = LazVerbStream(formula)
    except ValueError as e:
        return False, f'stream refused: {e}'
    cols = [c for c in vs.columns if c in order.columns]
    it = order[['match_id'] + cols].itertuples(index=True, name=None)
    got, idx = [], []
    for tup in it:
        ri, mid = tup[0], tup[1]
        rowmap = dict(zip(cols, tup[2:]))
        got.append(vs.update(mid, rowmap))
        idx.append(ri)
    b = pd.to_numeric(batch.loc[idx], errors='coerce').to_numpy(dtype='float64')
    s = np.asarray(got, dtype='float64')
    bn, sn = np.isnan(b), np.isnan(s)
    na_mismatch = int((bn != sn).sum())
    both = ~bn & ~sn
    inf_b, inf_s = np.isinf(b), np.isinf(s)
    inf_eq = (inf_b == inf_s) & (np.sign(np.where(inf_b, b, 0))
                                 == np.sign(np.where(inf_s, s, 0)))
    fin = both & ~inf_b & ~inf_s
    maxdiff = float(np.max(np.abs(b[fin] - s[fin]))) if fin.any() else 0.0
    ok = (na_mismatch == 0 and bool(inf_eq[both].all()) and maxdiff <= tol)
    info = (f'{len(s):,} rows | max |batch-stream| = {maxdiff:.2e} | '
            f'NaN-pattern mismatches = {na_mismatch}')
    if ok:
        LAZ_PORTED_VERBS[str(name)] = dict(
            formula=str(formula), rows=int(len(s)), max_diff=maxdiff,
            verified=_dt.datetime.now().isoformat(timespec='seconds'),
            engine=ENGINE_VERSION)
        _laz_ported_verbs_save()
        if verbose:
            print(f'[port] {name}: streaming replay VERIFIED — {info}')
    elif verbose:
        print(f'[port] {name}: streaming replay FAILED — {info}')
    return ok, info


def laz_build_proposed(df, name, formula, verbose=True):
    """Evaluate a proposed formula against the frame. Returns the Series, or None.

    [v75_61] Compiled through _laz_formula_series (ast, verb set, no eval). The sanity
    checks below are unchanged: coverage, finiteness, non-constancy.
    """
    if not formula or not isinstance(formula, str):
        return None
    out = _laz_formula_series(df, formula, name=name, verbose=verbose)
    if out is None:
        return None
    out = out.replace([np.inf, -np.inf], np.nan)
    if out.notna().mean() < 0.05:
        if verbose:
            print(f'[build] {name}: only {100*out.notna().mean():.1f}% of rows have a value '
                  f'— too sparse to support 100 bets in each window')
        return None
    if out.dropna().nunique() < 2:
        if verbose:
            print(f'[build] {name}: constant — selects everything or nothing')
        return None
    if verbose:
        _v = out.dropna()
        print(f'[build] {name}: computed, {100*out.notna().mean():.1f}% populated, '
              f'{_v.min():.4g}..{_v.max():.4g}')
    return out


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  POPULATION SEGMENTS — the proposer can now name a POPULATION, not only a feature
#  [v75_61]
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  The project's best segmentation win was hand-built: comeback1, football's trailing
#  market restricted to a ONE-goal deficit, after the whole-market average (+22.4%) was
#  shown to be a mixture of +27.9% and -57.2%. "An average hid a segment" is a lesson the
#  knowledge base records and the loop could not act on: it had no way to DEFINE a
#  population. Now it does. A segment is:
#      name          a new market name for the sport
#      base_market   an existing entry in LAZ_SEARCH_MARKETS for that sport
#      filter        a formula in the same safe language (arithmetic + the verb set);
#                    rows where filter > 0 are the population
#  Price and settlement are INHERITED from the base market -- a segment changes which
#  rows are searched, never how a bet pays. The filter is applied BEFORE the IS/FREEZE/
#  OOS windows are assigned, exactly as comeback1 does it, so the three windows are
#  35/30/35 of the population actually searched. Admitted segments persist beside the
#  ledger and appear in the evolve loop's market list.
LAZ_SEGMENTS_PATH = LAZ('laz_segments.json')
LAZ_PROPOSED_SEGMENTS = {}
try:
    import json as _sj
    with open(LAZ_SEGMENTS_PATH, encoding='utf-8') as _sf:
        LAZ_PROPOSED_SEGMENTS.update({k: v for k, v in _sj.load(_sf).items()
                                      if isinstance(v, dict)})
    if LAZ_PROPOSED_SEGMENTS:
        print(f'[segments] {len(LAZ_PROPOSED_SEGMENTS)} persisted segment(s) loaded')
except (FileNotFoundError, ValueError):
    pass
except Exception:
    pass


def laz_admit_segment(name, sport, base_market, filter_expr, hypothesis=None,
                      min_frac=0.02, max_frac=0.90, min_matches=150, verbose=True):
    """Gate a proposed population segment. Returns (admitted: bool, why: str).

    Structural checks only -- the search, the bar, the gates and the permutation
    control still rule on anything found inside the segment:
      1. the base market exists for this sport
      2. the filter compiles in the safe formula language and computes on the base frame
      3. it SELECTS a real subset: between min_frac and max_frac of the base ticks, on
         at least min_matches distinct matches -- a filter keeping everything is not a
         segment, and one keeping almost nothing cannot support 100 bets per window
    """
    import json, re as _r
    if not name or not _r.fullmatch(r'[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]{2,40}', str(name)):
        return False, 'segment name must be a plain identifier, 3-41 chars'
    if name in LAZ_SEARCH_MARKETS.get(sport, {}) or name in LAZ_PROPOSED_SEGMENTS:
        return False, f'{name} already exists as a market or segment'
    if base_market not in LAZ_SEARCH_MARKETS.get(sport, {}):
        return False, (f'base_market {base_market!r} is not a market for {sport}; '
                       f'known: {sorted(LAZ_SEARCH_MARKETS.get(sport, {}))}')
    try:
        base = _laz_search_frame(sport, base_market, verbose=False)
    except Exception as e:
        return False, f'base frame unavailable: {type(e).__name__}: {str(e)[:80]}'
    v = _laz_formula_series(base, filter_expr, name=f'{name}.filter', verbose=verbose)
    if v is None:
        return False, ('filter refused by the formula compiler — arithmetic over '
                       'column names plus the verbs '
                       + ', '.join(LAZ_FORMULA_VERBS) + ' only')
    sel = (pd.to_numeric(v, errors='coerce') > 0)
    frac = float(sel.mean()) if len(sel) else 0.0
    nmat = int(base.loc[sel, 'match_id'].nunique()) if ('match_id' in base.columns
                                                        and sel.any()) else 0
    if not (min_frac <= frac <= max_frac):
        return False, (f'filter keeps {100*frac:.1f}% of base ticks — a segment must '
                       f'keep {100*min_frac:.0f}%..{100*max_frac:.0f}%. Near 100% it is '
                       f'not a segment; near 0% it cannot support 100 bets per window.')
    if nmat < min_matches:
        return False, f'filter keeps only {nmat} distinct matches ({min_matches} needed)'
    LAZ_PROPOSED_SEGMENTS[name] = dict(sport=sport, base_market=base_market,
                                       filter=str(filter_expr),
                                       hypothesis=str(hypothesis or '')[:300],
                                       engine=ENGINE_VERSION)
    try:
        with open(LAZ_SEGMENTS_PATH, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as fh:
            json.dump(LAZ_PROPOSED_SEGMENTS, fh, indent=1, default=str)
    except OSError:
        pass
    if verbose:
        print(f'[segment] {sport}/{name}: {base_market} where ({filter_expr}) > 0 — '
              f'{100*frac:.1f}% of ticks, {nmat} matches. Now searchable.')
    return True, 'admitted'


def _laz_market_desc(sport, market):
    """The WHAT-IS-BACKED text for a market OR an admitted segment."""
    t = LAZ_SEARCH_MARKETS.get(sport, {}).get(market)
    if t:
        return t[1]
    s = LAZ_PROPOSED_SEGMENTS.get(market)
    if s and s.get('sport') == sport:
        base = LAZ_SEARCH_MARKETS.get(sport, {}).get(
            s.get('base_market'), ('', str(s.get('base_market', '?'))))[1]
        return f"{base} — SEGMENT: rows where ({s.get('filter')}) > 0"
    return '?'


def laz_production_spec(name, sport, formula=None, semantics=None, verbose=True):
    """[v75_104] OWNER LAW — the gate that documents itself out of existence:
    every new feature, at birth, writes its full production incorporation spec
    BEFORE it may build strategies. The spec follows the owner's formalized
    process (harvested from the 33 production scripts): namespace row +
    rt_allsports_laz_features column + state-machine compute. When the live
    gate later asks "can production replicate this?", the answer sheet
    already exists."""
    import os as _o
    try:
        d = _o.path.join(LAZARUS_HOME, 'laz_production_orders')
        _o.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
        p = _o.path.join(d, f'{sport}_{name}.md')
        voc = LAZ_PRODUCTION_TICK_VOCAB.get(
            'tennis' if sport == 'etennis' else sport, [])
        body = (
            f"# PRODUCTION INCORPORATION ORDER — {name} ({sport})\n\n"
            f"SEMANTICS: {semantics or '(as declared)'}\n"
            f"FORMULA (causal, over tick lineage): {formula or '(engineered in _laz_augment_frame — request port formula)'}\n\n"
            f"CHANGES REQUIRED, in order:\n"
            f"1. laz_feature_namespace: INSERT row (feature_name='{name}', sport='{sport}', semantics=..., causal='t')\n"
            f"2. rt_allsports_laz_features: ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN {name} double precision;\n"
            f"3. STATE-MACHINE COMPUTE: in <sport>_lazarus_efb_strategies, "
            f"PerMatchState.compute() — add '{name}' from the formula above, "
            f"using only live tick fields (backward-looking state + arithmetic)\n"
            f"4. Restart the sport watcher; the namespace fail-loud check verifies column+row agree\n"
            f"5. Engine side: triple-equality port (column == formula batch == stream, 1e-6) -> live gate PASS, awards release automatically\n\n"
            f"TICK FIELDS AVAILABLE ({len(voc)}): {', '.join(voc)}\n")
        with open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as fh:
            fh.write(body)
        if verbose:
            print(f'[production] spec written: {p}')
        return p
    except Exception as _e:
        if verbose:
            print(f'[production] spec write failed for {name}: {_e}')
        return None


def laz_admit_feature(name, sport, frame=None, engine_cls=None, min_coverage=0.05,
                      tol=1e-6, formula=None, verbose=True):
    """Gate a newly proposed feature. Returns (admitted: bool, report: dict)."""
    rep = dict(feature=name, sport=sport, checks={}, admitted=False, fix=None)

    def _fail(check, why, fix):
        rep['checks'][check] = f'FAIL — {why}'
        rep['fix'] = fix
        if verbose:
            print(f'[admit] {name} ({sport}): {check} FAIL — {why}')
            print(f'[admit]   fix: {fix}')
        return False, rep

    # ── 0. [v76_28] DEFINED, OR NOT ADMITTED ────────────────────────────────────────
    # The owner's rule: a new feature must be reverse-engineerable precisely.
    # A feature with no formula cannot be rebuilt in production, cannot be
    # replay-verified, and cannot be documented in Feature_Definitions -- so it
    # does not enter, however well it scores.
    _is_existing_col = False
    try:
        _is_existing_col = (frame is not None and name in frame.columns)
    except Exception:
        pass
    if (formula is None or not str(formula).strip()) and not _is_existing_col:
        return _fail('defined',
                     'no formula supplied and the name is not a frame column — '
                     'an undefined feature cannot be reverse-engineered into '
                     'production. (A feature that IS a CSV column needs no '
                     'formula; the export itself is the definition.)',
                     'resubmit with an explicit formula over frame columns, or '
                     'name an existing column exactly')
    # ── 1. CLASSIFIED ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    legal, phase, why = laz_feature_causal_class(name)
    if not legal and phase is None:
        return _fail('classified', why,
                     f"call laz_declare_feature({name!r}, semantics, definition) first. "
                     f"Classification is part of creating a feature, not a step afterwards: "
                     f"'row' (this tick alone), 'observed' (running stats over ticks already "
                     f"seen), 'anchor' (latched at or before the first tick), or 'phase' "
                     f"with resolves_at. An undeclared feature cannot be searched.")
    rep['checks']['classified'] = (f'ok — {why}' if phase is None
                                   else f'ok — phase-gated from {phase:g}')

    if frame is None:
        rep['checks']['computed'] = 'SKIPPED — no frame supplied'
        if verbose:
            print(f'[admit] {name}: classified only; pass frame= to run checks 2-5')
        return False, rep

    # ── 2. COMPUTED ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    try:
        out = _laz_augment_frame(frame.copy(), sport, needed={name}, verbose=False)
    except Exception as e:
        return _fail('computed', f'augment raised {type(e).__name__}: {str(e)[:70]}',
                     'the builder throws on this frame -- wrap it in its own try/except so '
                     'a failure costs this feature only, never the whole sport')
    if name not in out.columns and formula:
        # The proposer gave a formula -- evaluate it rather than refusing for the absence of
        # a hand-written builder. This is what lets a proposed feature be TESTED at all.
        _built = laz_build_proposed(out, name, formula, verbose=verbose)
        if _built is not None:
            out[name] = _built
            LAZ_PROPOSED_BUILDERS[name] = formula
    if name not in out.columns:
        return _fail('computed', 'not present in the augmented frame and no usable formula',
                     'give a formula using only column names the frame carries, or add a '
                     'builder for it in _laz_augment_frame under this sport')
    v = pd.to_numeric(out[name], errors='coerce')
    cov = float(v.notna().mean())
    if cov < min_coverage:
        return _fail('computed', f'only {100*cov:.1f}% of rows have a value',
                     'check the source columns exist on this feed; a feature present on '
                     'almost no rows cannot support 100 bets in each window')
    rep['checks']['computed'] = f'ok — {100*cov:.1f}% coverage'

    # ── 3. SANE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    fin = v.dropna()
    if not len(fin):
        return _fail('sane', 'every value is NaN', 'the builder produces nothing usable')
    if not np.isfinite(fin).all():
        return _fail('sane', 'contains inf', 'guard the divisions -- replace(0, np.nan)')
    if fin.nunique() < 2:
        return _fail('sane', f'constant at {fin.iloc[0]}',
                     'a constant column selects everything or nothing and will look like a '
                     'strategy while carrying no information')
    rep['checks']['sane'] = (f'ok — {fin.min():.4g}..{fin.max():.4g}, '
                             f'{fin.nunique()} distinct')

    # ── 4 & 5. LIVE AND EQUAL ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    if engine_cls is None:
        engine_cls = _LAZ_ENGINE_FOR_SPORT.get(sport)
    if engine_cls is None:
        rep['checks']['live'] = 'SKIPPED — no live engine class for this sport'
        rep['admitted'] = True
        if verbose:
            print(f'[admit] {name} ({sport}): ADMITTED (no live engine to replay against)')
        return True, rep

    try:
        mids = out['match_id'].dropna().unique()[:5]
        sub = out[out['match_id'].isin(mids)].sort_values(
            [c for c in ('match_id', 'ts') if c in out.columns])
        eng, last, live = None, None, []
        for _, r in sub.iterrows():
            if r['match_id'] != last:
                eng, last = engine_cls(), r['match_id']
            st = dict(r)
            if 'ts' in st and 'epoch_ts' not in st:
                try:
                    st['epoch_ts'] = pd.Timestamp(st['ts']).timestamp()
                except Exception:
                    pass
            st.setdefault('market_status', 'Open')
            try:
                live.append(eng.compute(st).get(name))
            except Exception:
                live.append(None)
        lv = pd.to_numeric(pd.Series(live), errors='coerce')
        bv = pd.to_numeric(sub[name], errors='coerce').reset_index(drop=True)
        if lv.notna().sum() == 0:
            # NOT YET PORTED is a task; NOT PORTABLE is a defect. Only the second is a
            # failure. A row/observed/anchor feature is mechanically portable by
            # construction, so the strategy reading it can be REGISTERED -- it just cannot
            # be staked until the port is written and replay confirms it.
            _st, _why = laz_feature_portability(name, sport)
            if _st in ('PORTABLE', 'PHASE_PORTABLE'):
                rep['checks']['live'] = f'PENDING — portable but not yet ported ({_why})'
                rep['checks']['equal'] = 'PENDING — cannot replay what is not yet emitted'
                rep['port_status'] = 'PENDING_LIVE_PORT'
                rep['admitted'] = True
                rep['fix'] = (f'add {name} to {engine_cls.__name__}.compute, then re-run '
                              f'this check so replay confirms 1e-6 on the same rows. The '
                              f'strategy may be registered now and staked after.')
                if verbose:
                    print(f'[admit] {name} ({sport}): ADMITTED PENDING PORT — {_why}')
                    print(f'[admit]   {rep["fix"]}')
                return True, rep
            return _fail('live', f'not portable: {_why}',
                         'this value cannot exist at the entry tick however it is coded')
        rep['checks']['live'] = f'ok — emitted on {lv.notna().sum():,} ticks'
        # Row COUNT is part of equality. ten_pm_ratio matched on every shared row and
        # still diverged, because the two columns covered different rows.
        n_lv, n_bv = int(lv.notna().sum()), int(bv.notna().sum())
        if abs(n_lv - n_bv) > max(5, 0.02 * max(n_lv, n_bv)):
            return _fail('equal', f'row counts differ: live {n_lv:,} vs backtest {n_bv:,}',
                         'the two paths cover different rows -- one is reading a different '
                         'source column or applying a filter the other does not')
        both = lv.notna() & bv.notna()
        d = float((lv[both] - bv[both]).abs().max()) if both.any() else float('nan')
        if not (d < tol):
            return _fail('equal', f'max divergence {d:.9f} over {int(both.sum()):,} ticks',
                         'reconcile the formulas -- the live and vectorised paths compute '
                         'different quantities, and the backtest number cannot be '
                         'reproduced in production')
        rep['checks']['equal'] = f'ok — max divergence {d:.9f}'
    except Exception as e:
        return _fail('equal', f'replay raised {type(e).__name__}: {str(e)[:70]}',
                     'the replay harness could not run -- check the engine class signature')

    rep['admitted'] = True
    if verbose:
        laz_production_spec(name, sport,
                            formula=(_fx if '_fx' in dir() else None),
                            verbose=verbose)
        print(f'[admit] {name} ({sport}): ADMITTED — ' +
              ' | '.join(f'{k} {v}' for k, v in rep['checks'].items()))
    return True, rep



# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  laz_halftime_features — THE HALF-TIME READ, MADE CAUSAL BY CONSTRUCTION      [v75_32]
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  The half-time score is real information and it is NOT forward-looking in the second
#  half: from the 46th minute it is as knowable as the current score, and it carries
#  something the current score does not -- whether the game changed shape at the break.
#  A side level at the interval and now a goal up is a different object from one that led
#  at the interval and still leads.
#
#  WHY THE RAW COLUMNS STAY BANNED. ht_h, ht_a and ht_diff are stored as OUTCOME COLUMNS:
#  written once at ingest and carried on every tick of the match, including the first
#  minute. There is no moment in the frame at which they "become" known -- they were never
#  absent. A phase gate on the ENTRY CONDITION cannot stop a stack from reading them early
#  if the condition is written loosely or the phase column is missing, and that is exactly
#  how forty football strategies came to report +53.0% ROI on no edge whatsoever.
#
#  SO THEY ARE NOT GATED -- THEY ARE REBUILT. Each raw column is masked to NaN before the
#  interval and published under an fb_ht_* name. Before half-time the value does not
#  exist, so a condition on it CANNOT fire, whatever the rest of the stack says. That is
#  causality by construction rather than by a rule someone has to remember, the same
#  discipline as laz_cp50_*.
#
#  PRODUCTION-EQUIVALENT. A live engine reproduces this by latching the score at the
#  interval and returning None before it -- one branch, the same shape as the tennis dog
#  role or the eBasketball quarter latch. Nothing here needs the match to finish.
#
#    fb_ht_h / fb_ht_a   goals at the interval, NaN in the first half
#    fb_ht_diff          the interval margin, home-signed
#    fb_ht_lead          1 home / -1 away / 0 level at the interval
#    fb_ht_fav_led       1.0 if the side we anchored as favourite led at the interval
#    fb_since_ht         how much the score has moved SINCE the interval -- the second-half
#                        story on its own, which the raw columns cannot express at all
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  laz_player_history — 70 COLUMNS OF PLAYER FORM THAT NOTHING HAS USED         [v75_34]
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  The eBasketball and eFootball feeds carry a complete player-history block, 91.8%
#  populated on the rows sampled, and no registered strategy touches any of it:
#
#    hp_total_player_wr / ap_total_player_wr    career win rate, 0..1, median 0.50
#    hp_winrate_l5g / l10g / l20g               rolling form over the last N games
#    hp_avggoals_scored_5 / _10 / _20           scoring rate over those windows
#    hp_avggoals_conceded_5 / _10 / _20         conceding rate
#    hp_h2h_winrate_l5g / _l10g                 record against THIS opponent
#    hp_h2h_avggoals_scored_l5g / conceded      goals in those meetings
#    hp_h2h_avg_matchggoals_l5g                 total goals when these two meet
#    hp_win_last2games_flag / last_games_flag   immediate momentum
#    betsson_home_player_elo                    the book's own rating, median 1500
#    home_player_total_games                    how much history the rating rests on
#  and the ap_ mirror of every one.
#
#  ALL ARE 'anchor'. Each is computed from matches that FINISHED BEFORE this one started
#  and is joined at ingest, constant for the whole match. Fixed before play, so legal at
#  every tick -- the same class as an opening price, and nothing here looks forward.
#
#  THE INTERACTION IS WHERE THE VALUE IS. A career win rate alone is weak; the market has
#  seen it too. What the market prices less well is the JOIN between the pre-match role and
#  the form behind it: a favourite whose favouritism rests on a 0.50 career rate is a
#  different object from one backed by 0.70, and the live price does not always separate
#  them. So the derived block below carries the role-relative reads --
#      laz_fav_wr        the win rate of the side the market made favourite
#      laz_wr_gap        favourite's win rate minus the underdog's -- how much of the
#                        price gap the form actually supports
#      laz_fav_wr_vs_px  that gap against the price gap. High values mean the market has
#                        NOT fully priced the form difference; low or negative means it has
#                        priced more than the form supports.
#      laz_h2h_edge      the favourite's head-to-head record against THIS opponent, which
#                        is a different question from its record overall
#      laz_form_swing    recent form (l5g) minus career -- a player above or below their
#                        own baseline right now
#  Those five are the shape the owner described: take the favourite, join its player
#  history, and find the interval where the history actually moves the outcome.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

def laz_player_features(df, sport, verbose=True):
    """Role-relative player history: the favourite's form, not the home side's."""
    try:
        if not any(c.startswith(('hp_', 'ap_')) for c in df.columns):
            return df
        fav_home = pd.to_numeric(df.get('laz_pm_fav_home'), errors='coerce')
        if fav_home is None or fav_home.isna().all():
            return df
        fh = fav_home > 0

        def _pair(base):
            h = pd.to_numeric(df.get('hp_' + base), errors='coerce')
            a = pd.to_numeric(df.get('ap_' + base), errors='coerce')
            if h is None or a is None:
                return None, None
            # Favourite's value and underdog's value, whichever side each happens to be.
            return (pd.Series(np.where(fh, h, a), index=df.index),
                    pd.Series(np.where(fh, a, h), index=df.index))

        added = 0
        wr_f, wr_d = _pair('total_player_wr')
        if wr_f is not None:
            df['laz_fav_wr'] = wr_f
            df['laz_dog_wr'] = wr_d
            # How much of the price gap the FORM actually supports.
            df['laz_wr_gap'] = wr_f - wr_d
            added += 3
            _ho = pd.to_numeric(df.get('home_odds'), errors='coerce')
            _ao = pd.to_numeric(df.get('away_odds'), errors='coerce')
            if _ho is not None and _ao is not None:
                _mid = df['match_id']
                _h0 = _mid.map(_ho.groupby(_mid).first())
                _a0 = _mid.map(_ao.groupby(_mid).first())
                _px_gap = (1 / np.minimum(_h0, _a0).replace(0, np.nan)
                           - 1 / np.maximum(_h0, _a0).replace(0, np.nan))
                # Form gap against price gap. High = the market has NOT priced the form
                # difference; low or negative = it has priced more than the form supports.
                df['laz_fav_wr_vs_px'] = df['laz_wr_gap'] / _px_gap.replace(0, np.nan)
                added += 1
        h2h_f, _ = _pair('h2h_winrate_l5g')
        if h2h_f is not None:
            # The favourite's record against THIS opponent -- a different question from its
            # record overall, and the one the price is least likely to carry.
            df['laz_h2h_edge'] = h2h_f
            added += 1
        f5, _ = _pair('winrate_l5g')
        if f5 is not None and 'laz_fav_wr' in df.columns:
            # Above or below their own baseline right now.
            df['laz_form_swing'] = f5 - df['laz_fav_wr']
            added += 1
        eh = pd.to_numeric(df.get('betsson_home_player_elo'), errors='coerce')
        ea = pd.to_numeric(df.get('betsson_away_player_elo'), errors='coerce')
        if eh is not None and ea is not None and not eh.isna().all():
            df['laz_fav_elo'] = np.where(fh, eh, ea)
            df['laz_elo_gap'] = np.where(fh, eh - ea, ea - eh)
            added += 2
        gh = pd.to_numeric(df.get('home_player_total_games'), errors='coerce')
        ga = pd.to_numeric(df.get('away_player_total_games'), errors='coerce')
        if gh is not None and ga is not None and not gh.isna().all():
            # How much history the favourite's rating rests on. A 0.70 win rate over 12
            # games and over 400 are not the same claim.
            df['laz_fav_games'] = np.where(fh, gh, ga)
            added += 1
        mg = pd.to_numeric(df.get('hp_h2h_avg_matchggoals_l5g'), errors='coerce')
        if mg is not None and not mg.isna().all():
            # Total goals when these two meet -- the totals-market read, from history.
            df['laz_h2h_goals'] = mg
            added += 1
        if verbose and added:
            _n = int(df['laz_fav_wr'].notna().sum()) if 'laz_fav_wr' in df.columns else 0
            print(f'[player] {sport}: {added} role-relative feature(s) from the player '
                  f'history block | {_n:,} ticks with a favourite win rate')
    except Exception as _pe:
        if verbose:
            print(f'[player] {sport}: skipped ({type(_pe).__name__}: {str(_pe)[:60]})')
    return df


def laz_halftime_features(df, sport, verbose=True):
    """Half-time state, NaN before the interval. Second half only, by construction."""
    try:
        mid = df['match_id']
        # Is this tick after the interval? Prefer the explicit flag; fall back to the
        # phase string, then to progress past the midpoint.
        if 'is_second_half' in df.columns:
            h2 = pd.to_numeric(df['is_second_half'], errors='coerce').fillna(0) > 0
        elif 'phase' in df.columns:
            h2 = df['phase'].astype(str).str.contains('2nd|Second|Extra',
                                                      case=False, regex=True, na=False)
        else:
            _pr = pd.to_numeric(df.get('fb_prog', df.get('prog')), errors='coerce')
            if _pr is None or _pr.isna().all():
                return df
            h2 = _pr > 0.5
        if not h2.any():
            return df
        hs = pd.to_numeric(df.get('h_score'), errors='coerce')
        as_ = pd.to_numeric(df.get('a_score'), errors='coerce')
        if hs is None or hs.isna().all():
            return df

        # Prefer the stored interval score where the feed carries it; otherwise take the
        # last first-half tick. Either way the RESULT is masked to the second half.
        if {'ht_h', 'ht_a'}.issubset(df.columns):
            _hh = pd.to_numeric(df['ht_h'], errors='coerce')
            _ha = pd.to_numeric(df['ht_a'], errors='coerce')
        else:
            _hh = hs.where(~h2).groupby(mid).transform('last')
            _ha = as_.where(~h2).groupby(mid).transform('last')

        # THE MASK IS THE WHOLE POINT. Before the interval these are NaN, so a condition on
        # them cannot fire -- no gate, no rule, no memory required.
        df['fb_ht_h'] = _hh.where(h2)
        df['fb_ht_a'] = _ha.where(h2)
        _hd = (_hh - _ha).where(h2)
        df['fb_ht_diff'] = _hd
        df['fb_ht_lead'] = np.sign(_hd)
        _fav_home = pd.to_numeric(df.get('laz_pm_fav_home'), errors='coerce')
        if _fav_home is not None and not _fav_home.isna().all():
            df['fb_ht_fav_led'] = pd.Series(
                np.where(_hd.isna() | (_hd == 0), 0.0,
                         np.where(_fav_home > 0, _hd > 0, _hd < 0).astype(float)),
                index=df.index).where(h2)
        # The second-half story on its own: what the raw columns cannot express.
        df['fb_since_ht'] = ((hs - as_) - _hd).where(h2)
        if verbose:
            _n = int(df['fb_ht_diff'].notna().sum())
            print(f'[halftime] {sport}: 6 feature(s), NaN before the interval | '
                  f'{_n:,} second-half ticks')
    except Exception as _he:
        if verbose:
            print(f'[halftime] {sport}: skipped ({type(_he).__name__}: {str(_he)[:60]})')
    return df


def laz_checkpoint_features(df, sport, verbose=True):
    """Latch match state at fixed fractions of progress, readable only afterwards."""
    try:
        prog_col = next((c for c in ('fb_prog', 'prog_score', 'prog', 'efb_prog',
                                     'bb_prog_score', 'ebb_prog', 'ten_prog')
                         if c in df.columns), None)
        pr = None
        if prog_col is not None:
            pr = pd.to_numeric(df[prog_col], errors='coerce')
        if pr is None or pr.isna().all():
            # FALL BACK TO TICK POSITION. Not every sport carries a progress column at this
            # stage -- basketball builds prog_score later, tennis has none at all -- and the
            # checkpoint family is worth having on every sport. Rank within the match gives
            # the same 0..1 axis from data every frame has, and it is causal: it is computed
            # from ticks already seen, never from the match's eventual length.
            pr = df.groupby('match_id').cumcount().astype('float64')
            _tot = df.groupby('match_id')['match_id'].transform('size').astype('float64')
            pr = pr / np.maximum(_tot - 1, 1)
            prog_col = '<tick position>'
        mid = df['match_id']
        hs = pd.to_numeric(df.get('h_score'), errors='coerce')
        as_ = pd.to_numeric(df.get('a_score'), errors='coerce')
        if hs is None or as_ is None or hs.isna().all():
            return df
        sd = hs - as_
        fav_home = pd.to_numeric(df.get('laz_pm_fav_home'), errors='coerce')
        added = 0
        for cp in LAZ_CHECKPOINTS:
            tag = f'laz_cp{int(cp*100)}'
            # The LAST tick at or before the checkpoint -- the state as it stood there.
            at = sd.where(pr <= cp).groupby(mid).transform('last')
            # NaN until the match has actually passed the checkpoint, so a condition on it
            # cannot fire earlier. This is what makes the family causal by construction.
            seen = (pr >= cp)
            df[f'{tag}_sd'] = at.where(seen)
            df[f'{tag}_lead'] = np.sign(at).where(seen)
            if fav_home is not None and not fav_home.isna().all():
                _fav_ahead = np.where(fav_home > 0, at > 0, at < 0)
                df[f'{tag}_fav_lead'] = pd.Series(
                    np.where(at.isna() | (at == 0), 0.0, _fav_ahead.astype(float)),
                    index=df.index).where(seen)
            df[f'{tag}_swing'] = (sd - at).where(seen)
            added += 4
        if verbose:
            _c = f'laz_cp50_sd'
            _n = int(df[_c].notna().sum()) if _c in df.columns else 0
            print(f'[checkpoints] {sport}: {added} feature(s) at '
                  f'{", ".join(f"{int(c*100)}%" for c in LAZ_CHECKPOINTS)} of progress | '
                  f'{_n:,} ticks past the 50% mark')
    except Exception as _ce:
        if verbose:
            print(f'[checkpoints] {sport}: skipped '
                  f'({type(_ce).__name__}: {str(_ce)[:60]})')
    return df


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  FRAME CACHE — the single biggest cost in the loop                            [v75_53]
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  _laz_search_frame reads the whole parquet file, augments it, attaches price and
#  settlement, and splits it into three windows. On the owner's data that is 4.56M rows for
#  tennis and 3.76M for football, and it ran ONCE PER ROUND -- so a 200-restart round took
#  over an hour on a machine sitting at 99% CPU. The search itself was never the cost; the
#  preparation was, repeated twenty times over identical inputs.
#
#  The prepared frame depends only on (sport, market, min_odds) and the parquet's mtime.
#  Nothing inside a round changes any of those, so it can be built once and reused. That
#  turns a round from hours into minutes, which is what makes 2000 restarts affordable --
#  and 2000 is the difference between finding the twelve BBQ stacks and finding nothing.
#
#  KEYED ON mtime, so a rebuilt parquet invalidates the entry rather than serving stale
#  data. Capped at two entries: these frames are hundreds of MB and the 16 GB machines
#  cannot hold five. The cap evicts the oldest, which on a loop that alternates between two
#  markets is the right behaviour.
_LAZ_FRAME_CACHE = {}
_LAZ_FRAME_CACHE_MAX = 2


def laz_frame_cache_clear(verbose=True):
    """Drop every cached frame. Call after rebuilding a parquet mid-session."""
    _n = len(_LAZ_FRAME_CACHE)
    _LAZ_FRAME_CACHE.clear()
    if verbose and _n:
        print(f'[cache] cleared {_n} prepared frame(s)')


def _laz_feed_complete_filter(sport, market, df, verbose=True):
    """[v75_76] THE bb66 LAW, ported to eSports: a dead feed proves nothing.
    The v66 session measured it on real basketball — complete feeds 55.7%
    under-wins, truncated 69.9%, dead-before-Q4 84.8%: truncation manufactures
    winners. eSports frames carry no completeness flag, so it is judged here,
    from the last recorded tick per match: ebasketball must reach the 4th
    quarter, efootball the 2nd half. Matches whose feed died earlier are
    excluded from the searchable universe entirely — their finals are
    unproven, so nothing may settle on them. If the frame lacks the columns
    to judge, the filter says so out loud instead of pretending."""
    if (sport not in ('efootball', 'ebasketball', 'football', 'basketball')
            or df is None or not len(df) or 'match_id' not in df.columns):
        return df
    if 'ts' in df.columns:
        _last = df.loc[df.groupby('match_id', observed=True)['ts'].idxmax()]
    else:
        _last = df.groupby('match_id', as_index=False).tail(1)
    ok = None
    if sport == 'ebasketball' and 'q' in df.columns:
        _qv = pd.to_numeric(_last['q'], errors='coerce')
        if _qv.notna().mean() > 0.9:
            ok = _qv >= 4
    if ok is None and 'phase' in df.columns:
        _ph = _last['phase'].astype(str).str.lower()
        # [v75_85] OWNER LAW: 'Fulltime'/'Overtime' labels carry ZERO weight —
        # bookmakers pull markets on blowouts long before such labels print, so
        # they appear for too few matches to validate or gate anything, ever.
        # Completeness = the feed reached the final period. Nothing else.
        if sport in ('ebasketball', 'basketball'):
            ok = _ph.str.contains('4th', na=False)
        elif sport == 'efootball':
            ok = _ph.str.contains('2nd half', na=False)
        else:  # football
            _mins = _ph.str.extract(r'(\d+) minute')[0].astype(float)
            ok = _mins >= 88
    if ok is None:
        if verbose:
            print(f'[feed] {sport}/{market}: no phase/q columns — completeness '
                  f'cannot be judged; filter honestly skipped')
        return df
    _dead = set(_last.loc[~ok.values, 'match_id'])
    if _dead:
        df = df[~df['match_id'].isin(_dead)].copy()
        if verbose:
            print(f'[feed] {sport}/{market}: {len(_dead)} match(es) with truncated '
                  f'feeds excluded — a dead feed proves nothing (bb66 law)')
    return df


def _laz_esports_lockout(sport, market, df, verbose=True):
    """[v75_74] ESPORTS LATE-ENTRY LOCKOUT — an owner LAW, structural.
    Past ~80% of an eSports match the bookmaker has usually suspended the market,
    whatever the feed's last quoted price claims; a strategy entering there is a
    fiction. Applied to the prepared frame on EVERY return path, so the search,
    the tick:K counter, the evidence replay and the metrics share one world in
    which late ticks do not exist. Basis: the engineered progress column when it
    is a true 0..1 fraction, else each match's recorded-timeline fraction."""
    # [v75_102] OWNER LAW: tennis — eTennis included — is SETS-based, no
    # clock, no late suspension pattern; the 80% lockout does not apply.
    if sport not in ('efootball', 'ebasketball') or df is None \
            or not len(df):
        return df
    # [v75_80] The rule is an eSPORTS rule: within the mixed tennis frame it
    # binds ONLY rows flagged is_etennis; real tennis (and all real sports)
    # keep their full timeline. A tennis frame without the flag is untouched.
    if sport == 'tennis':
        if 'is_etennis' not in df.columns or 'match_id' not in df.columns \
                or 'ts' not in df.columns:
            return df
        _gts = df.groupby('match_id')['ts']
        _fr = _gts.rank(method='first') / _gts.transform('size')
        _lk = (_fr > 0.80) & (pd.to_numeric(df['is_etennis'], errors='coerce') > 0)
        if bool(_lk.any()):
            _nlk = int(_lk.sum())
            df = df.loc[~_lk].copy()
            if verbose:
                print(f'[lockout] tennis/{market}: {_nlk:,} eTennis tick(s) past 80% '
                      f'removed — real-tennis rows untouched')
        return df
    _lk = None
    _pc = next((c for c in ('efb_prog', 'ebb_prog', 'prog') if c in df.columns), None)
    if _pc is not None:
        _pv = pd.to_numeric(df[_pc], errors='coerce')
        if _pv.notna().mean() > 0.98 and _pv.dropna().between(-0.001, 1.02).mean() > 0.99:
            _lk = _pv > 0.80
    if _lk is None and 'match_id' in df.columns and 'ts' in df.columns:
        _gts = df.groupby('match_id')['ts']
        _lk = (_gts.rank(method='first') / _gts.transform('size')) > 0.80
    if _lk is not None and bool(_lk.any()):
        _nlk = int(_lk.sum())
        df = df.loc[~_lk].copy()
        if verbose:
            print(f'[lockout] {sport}/{market}: {_nlk:,} tick(s) past 80% of the '
                  f'match timeline removed — eSports books lock late; entries '
                  f'there would be fiction')
    return df


def _laz_decat(df):
    """[v75_101] CATEGORICAL SEAL, every loader: parquet Categoricals leave
    ghost categories after the law filters drop rows, crashing groupby with
    "Can't get idxmax of an empty group due to unobserved categories".
    Caught by the owner on basketball AND football (same scraper family).
    Strings carry no ghosts — applied at every frame entry point."""
    try:
        for _cc in df.columns:
            if str(df[_cc].dtype) == 'category':
                df[_cc] = df[_cc].astype(str)
    except Exception:
        pass
    return df


def _laz_match_q(df, ts):
    """[v75_102] WINDOW SPLIT BY MATCH, not by tick. The owner's books proved
    tick-ranking degenerate: backfilled history holds few ticks per match,
    live scraping hundreds, so 35% of TICKS swallowed thousands of early
    matches into IS while OOS got a handful (13,304 vs 1,074 bets). Ranking
    whole matches by their first timestamp gives every window an equal share
    of MATCHES — the unit bets are made of — and no match ever straddles a
    window boundary."""
    try:
        starts = df.groupby('match_id', observed=True)[ts].min()
        r = starts.rank(pct=True, method='average')
        return df['match_id'].map(r)
    except Exception:
        return df[ts].rank(pct=True)


def _laz_search_frame(sport, market, min_odds=None, verbose=True, use_cache=True):
    # [v75_126] None -> the owner's per-bet floor. Explicit callers still win.
    min_odds = LAZ_MIN_ODDS if min_odds is None else min_odds
    # [v76_11] HP_/AP_ -> hp_/ap_ before anything reads a column name.
    """Load the sport's frame, augment it, attach price and settlement for `market`."""
    import os
    try:
        laz_restore_declared(verbose=False)   # [v75_61] self-heal: declared
    except Exception:                          # features exist before any frame
        pass                                   # is prepared, in every process
    p = os.path.join(FRAMES_DIR, f'AB_{sport}.parquet')
    if not os.path.exists(p):
        raise FileNotFoundError(f'no frame for {sport}: run the engine on it first')
    # Cache key includes the parquet's mtime, so a rebuild invalidates rather than serving a
    # frame prepared from data that no longer exists.
    _key = (sport, market, round(float(min_odds), 4), os.path.getmtime(p))
    if use_cache and _key in _LAZ_FRAME_CACHE:
        _hit = _LAZ_FRAME_CACHE[_key]
        if verbose:
            print(f'[cache] {sport}/{market}: reusing prepared frame '
                  f'({len(_hit):,} ticks) — no re-read, no re-augment')
        return _hit
    df = _laz_decat(pd.read_parquet(p))
    # [v75_100] CATEGORICAL SEAL: parquet can store id/phase columns as
    # Categoricals; after the law filters drop rows, ghost categories of
    # removed matches make groupby raise "Can't get idxmax of an empty
    # group due to unobserved categories" and the sport burns rounds doing
    # nothing (owner's basketball log, rounds 168+). Strings carry no ghosts.
    for _cc in df.columns:
        if str(df[_cc].dtype) == 'category':
            df[_cc] = df[_cc].astype(str)
    try:
        want = {c.split()[0] for x in laz_registry().get(sport, [])
                for c in x.get('conditions', []) if isinstance(c, str)}
        df = _laz_augment_frame(df, sport, needed=want, verbose=False)
        # ── [v76_47] CLEAR LEVEL-SCORE CONTAMINATION *AFTER* THE AUGMENT ─────
        # Doing this inside _laz_augment_frame did not work and the reason is
        # worth writing down: the augment REPLAYS the live engine, which
        # recomputes cb_trail_by / leader_* / loser_* for every row -- refilling
        # exactly the cells that had just been cleared. The clear therefore has
        # to be the LAST thing that touches the frame before the guard reads it.
        # At a level score there is no leader and no loser, so a lead-dependent
        # value there came from a different tick than the price: that is
        # lookahead, and it was blocking efootball/over and every 1X2 market.
        try:
            if {'h_score', 'a_score'}.issubset(df.columns):
                _lv = (pd.to_numeric(df['h_score'], errors='coerce')
                       == pd.to_numeric(df['a_score'], errors='coerce')).fillna(False)
                if _lv.any():
                    _lc = [c for c in df.columns
                           if re.search(r'(^|_)(cb_|trail|loser|leader|lead_)',
                                        str(c), re.I)
                           and pd.api.types.is_numeric_dtype(df[c])]
                    if _lc:
                        df = df.copy()
                        # INTEGER COLUMNS CANNOT HOLD NaN. The previous cut
                        # assigned NaN straight into int64 columns, pandas
                        # raised, and the surrounding `except: pass` swallowed
                        # it -- so the clear silently did nothing three times
                        # over. Cast to float first, then clear.
                        for _c48 in _lc:
                            if not pd.api.types.is_float_dtype(df[_c48]):
                                df[_c48] = df[_c48].astype('float64')
                        df.loc[_lv, _lc] = np.nan
                        if verbose:
                            print(f'[search] {sport}: cleared {len(_lc)} '
                                  f'lead-dependent column(s) at {int(_lv.sum()):,} '
                                  f'level-score tick(s) — they cannot exist there')
        except Exception:
            pass
    except Exception:
        pass
    # ── REBUILD PROPOSER FORMULAS HERE ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # A formula admitted in an earlier round has to exist on the frame the NEXT search reads,
    # or the pool cannot put thresholds on it and the proposal is admitted into nothing. The
    # earlier attempt rebuilt them inside _laz_augment_frame, which the search path does not
    # always go through -- so the column was missing exactly where it was needed.
    for _pn, _pf in list(LAZ_PROPOSED_BUILDERS.items()):
        if _pn not in df.columns:
            try:
                _b = laz_build_proposed(df, _pn, _pf, verbose=False)
                if _b is not None:
                    df[_pn] = _b
            except Exception:
                pass

    mk = LAZ_SEARCH_MARKETS.get(sport, {}).get(market)
    # ── [v75_61] ADMITTED SEGMENTS ARE MARKETS ────────────────────────────────────────
    # A segment inherits the base market's price and settlement and applies its filter
    # BEFORE the windows are assigned -- the comeback1 pattern, generalised. The window
    # re-rank matters: 35/30/35 must describe the population actually searched.
    if mk is None and market in LAZ_PROPOSED_SEGMENTS \
            and LAZ_PROPOSED_SEGMENTS[market].get('sport') == sport:
        _seg = LAZ_PROPOSED_SEGMENTS[market]
        base = _laz_search_frame(sport, _seg['base_market'], min_odds=min_odds,
                                 verbose=False, use_cache=use_cache)
        _fv = _laz_formula_series(base, _seg['filter'], name=f'{market}.filter',
                                  verbose=False)
        if _fv is None:
            raise ValueError(f'{sport}/{market}: segment filter no longer computes on '
                             f'the base frame — re-admit it')
        out = base[pd.to_numeric(_fv, errors='coerce') > 0].copy()
        _ts = next((c for c in ('mstart', 'ts') if c in out.columns), None)
        if _ts:
            out = out.sort_values(_ts)
            _q = _laz_match_q(out, _ts)
            out = out.assign(_win=np.where(_q <= 0.35, 'IS',
                                           np.where(_q <= 0.65, 'FRZ', 'OOS')))
        if verbose:
            print(f'[search] {sport}/{market}: SEGMENT of {_seg["base_market"]} — '
                  f'{len(out):,} of {len(base):,} ticks where ({_seg["filter"]}) > 0')
        if use_cache:
            while len(_LAZ_FRAME_CACHE) >= _LAZ_FRAME_CACHE_MAX:
                _LAZ_FRAME_CACHE.pop(next(iter(_LAZ_FRAME_CACHE)))
            _LAZ_FRAME_CACHE[_key] = out
        return out
    if mk is None:
        raise ValueError(f'{sport} has no market {market!r}; known: '
                         f'{sorted(LAZ_SEARCH_MARKETS.get(sport, {}))}'
                         + (f' + segments {sorted(n for n, s in LAZ_PROPOSED_SEGMENTS.items() if s.get("sport") == sport)}'
                            if any(s.get('sport') == sport
                                   for s in LAZ_PROPOSED_SEGMENTS.values()) else ''))
    col, _desc = mk
    # ── PRICE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # comeback1 is trail_ml restricted to a ONE-GOAL deficit. Same price and settlement,
    # a different population -- so it shares the branch and adds one filter at the end.
    _mkt_eff = 'trail_ml' if market == 'comeback1' else market
    if _mkt_eff in ('home', 'draw', 'away'):
        # [v76_47] Price is the side's own column at the tick; the bet wins only
        # if final_result names that side. The tick scoreboard decides nothing --
        # reading it is the circular settlement that once produced 100% strike
        # on 332 moneyline bets.
        _pc3 = {'home': 'home_odds', 'draw': 'draw_odds', 'away': 'away_odds'}[_mkt_eff]
        _rc3 = next((c for c in ('final_result', 'final_winner') if c in df.columns), None)
        if _pc3 not in df.columns or _rc3 is None:
            raise ValueError(f'{sport}/{market}: needs {_pc3} and a final_result '
                             f'column to settle a match-result bet')
        o = pd.to_numeric(df[_pc3], errors='coerce')
        _fr3 = (df[_rc3].astype(str).str.strip().str.lower()
                .str.replace('_team', '', regex=False))
        won = pd.Series(np.where(_fr3.isin(['home', 'draw', 'away']),
                                 (_fr3 == _mkt_eff).astype(float), np.nan),
                        index=df.index)
    elif _mkt_eff in ('lead_ml', 'trail_ml') and {'h_score', 'a_score'}.issubset(df.columns):
        _sd = (pd.to_numeric(df['h_score'], errors='coerce')
               - pd.to_numeric(df['a_score'], errors='coerce'))
        _ho = pd.to_numeric(df.get('home_odds'), errors='coerce')
        _ao = pd.to_numeric(df.get('away_odds'), errors='coerce')
        lead = _mkt_eff == 'lead_ml'
        o = pd.Series(np.where(_sd > 0, (_ho if lead else _ao),
                               np.where(_sd < 0, (_ao if lead else _ho), np.nan)),
                      index=df.index)
        # ── SETTLE AGAINST THE MATCH RESULT, NOT AGAINST THE SCOREBOARD ───────────────
        # The first version of this line read:
        #     won = np.where(_sd > 0, (_sd > 0) if lead else (_sd < 0), np.nan)
        # which asks "is the home side ahead?" and then answers "yes, the home side is
        # ahead" -- circular, and it never compares to the outcome at all. Every leading-ml
        # bet scored as a WIN, so the search reported stacks at 100.0% strike on 332 bets
        # and the permutation control found 98 of 116 in shuffled data. Both numbers are
        # impossible and that is how it was caught.
        # The bet wins only if the side backed AT THE ENTRY TICK is the side that WINS THE
        # MATCH. That requires the final result, which the frame carries as final_result or
        # final_winner and which this branch previously ignored.
        _res_col = next((c for c in ('final_result', 'final_winner', 'final_result_settled')
                         if c in df.columns), None)
        if _res_col is None:
            raise ValueError(f'{sport}/{market}: no final_result column -- cannot settle a '
                             f'moneyline bet without knowing who won the match')
        _fr = df[_res_col].astype(str).str.strip().str.lower()
        _fr = _fr.str.replace('_team', '', regex=False)
        _backed = pd.Series(np.where(_sd > 0, ('home' if lead else 'away'),
                                     np.where(_sd < 0, ('away' if lead else 'home'), '')),
                            index=df.index)
        won = pd.Series(np.where(_backed == '', np.nan, (_fr == _backed).astype(float)),
                        index=df.index)
    elif col in df.columns:
        o = pd.to_numeric(df[col], errors='coerce')
        won = None
    elif market in ('winner', 'live_lead'):
        # Tennis has no single price column: which side is backed depends on who won set 1,
        # so the price is home_odds or away_odds per row. Resolved in the settlement block
        # below, which also knows the side. Placeholder here.
        # [v76_9] live_lead resolves the same way — the side comes from odds drift.
        o, won = None, None
    else:
        raise ValueError(f'{sport}: price column {col!r} not in the frame; the frame has '
                         f'{[c for c in df.columns if "odds" in c or "under" in c][:6]}')
    # ── SETTLEMENT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # ── HANDICAP SETTLEMENT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # A spread bet wins when the backed side's FINAL MARGIN beats the line. Signed from the
    # backed side's point of view: a home bet on -4.5 needs the home side to win by 5, an
    # away bet on +4.5 survives a 4-point loss. The line is the handicap in force at the
    # entry tick, not at the close, because that is what the price was quoted against.
    if market in ('hcap_home', 'hcap_away'):
        _fh = next((c for c in ('final_h_score', 'final_home', 'final_h') if c in df.columns),
                   None)
        _fa = next((c for c in ('final_a_score', 'final_away', 'final_a') if c in df.columns),
                   None)
        _hc = next((c for c in ('point_spread_handicap', 'goal_spread_handicap',
                                'spread_handicap', 'handicap') if c in df.columns), None)
        if _fh and _fa and _hc:
            _mh = (pd.to_numeric(df[_fh], errors='coerce')
                   - pd.to_numeric(df[_fa], errors='coerce'))
            _line = pd.to_numeric(df[_hc], errors='coerce')
            # [v75_116] SETTLEMENT BUG — the handicap line was applied with the
            # SAME sign to both sides, so for a line of +2.5 BOTH home and away
            # "covered" whenever the margin fell inside the spread. That is
            # impossible in a real book: exactly one side covers. It produced
            # basketball hcap_away stacks at 99.7-100% strike at odds ~2.05
            # (owner's log, 20 Aug) — phantom ★★★, not edge. The line is the
            # HOME handicap; away is its exact negation.
            # [v75_121] SIGN DERIVED, NOT ASSUMED. Measured on the owner's own
            # frames (2,020 bets, 7 starred stacks): point_spread_handicap is an
            # UNSIGNED MAGNITUDE laid against whichever side leads at the entry
            # tick -- it correlates 0.921 with the away lead and never goes
            # negative (min 2.5). v75_116 applied it as though it were signed
            # home-minus-away, which is right only while the AWAY side leads and
            # wrong the moment the HOME side does. The favourite lays the points;
            # the underdog receives them. Which is which is read from the score
            # at the bet, never assumed.
            _hs = pd.to_numeric(df.get('h_score'), errors='coerce')
            _as = pd.to_numeric(df.get('a_score'), errors='coerce')
            _m0 = _hs - _as                       # home margin AT THE BET TICK
            _laid_on_home = _m0 > 0               # home leads -> home lays the line
            _laid_on_away = _m0 < 0
            _abs = _line.abs()
            if market == 'hcap_home':
                # home lays -abs when it leads, receives +abs when it trails
                won = np.where(_laid_on_home, (_mh - _abs) > 0,
                      np.where(_laid_on_away, (_mh + _abs) > 0, np.nan))
            else:
                _ma = -_mh                        # away margin at the final
                won = np.where(_laid_on_away, (_ma - _abs) > 0,
                      np.where(_laid_on_home, (_ma + _abs) > 0, np.nan))
            # Level at the entry tick -> nobody is laying the line. Refusing to
            # settle is the honest answer; the row is dropped by the notna()
            # filter below rather than guessed at.
            won = pd.Series(won, index=df.index).astype('float64')
            _LAZ_HCAP_SIGN_DERIVED = True
            df = df.assign(_o=o, _won=won.astype('float64'))
            df['_pnl'] = np.where(df['_won'] > 0, df['_o'] - 1.0, -1.0)
            _ts = next((c for c in ('mstart', 'ts') if c in df.columns), None)
            df = df[df['_o'].notna() & df['_won'].notna() & (df['_o'] >= min_odds)]
            # [v75_102] LAWS BEFORE WINDOWS — owner's catch: the split used to
            # run BEFORE feed-death/lockout, and the unsettled matches those
            # laws remove are the NEWEST data, i.e. exactly the OOS half.
            # Result: 1,000 IS bets vs 0 OOS. Windows are now cut on the
            # SURVIVING rows — two honest halves of usable data, always.
            df = _laz_feed_complete_filter(sport, market, df, verbose=verbose)
            df = _laz_esports_lockout(sport, market, df, verbose=verbose)
            if _ts:
                df = df.sort_values(_ts)
                # [v75_131] RANK MATCHES, NOT TICKS. This one split site used a raw
                # tick rank while the other two already used _laz_match_q. A match
                # contributes as many rows as it has qualifying ticks, so a period
                # of dense ticks floods whichever window it lands in. Measured on
                # the owner's book: LZ_EBA_under_EbbLine_ac4c16 came out IS 4,478 /
                # OOS 135 / FREEZE 2,870 -- an out-of-sample window of 1.8%, on
                # 7,483 bets. Ranking by MATCH START gives every match one vote and
                # restores the intended 35 / 30 / 35.
                _q = _laz_match_q(df, _ts)
                df = df.assign(_win=np.where(_q <= 0.35, 'IS',
                                             np.where(_q <= 0.65, 'FRZ', 'OOS')))
            else:
                df = df.assign(_win='IS')
            if verbose:
                print(f'[search] {sport}/{market}: {len(df):,} placeable ticks | '
                      f'strike {100*df["_won"].mean():.1f}% | '
                      f'median odds {df["_o"].median():.2f} | spread market')
            if use_cache:
                while len(_LAZ_FRAME_CACHE) >= _LAZ_FRAME_CACHE_MAX:
                    _LAZ_FRAME_CACHE.pop(next(iter(_LAZ_FRAME_CACHE)))
                _LAZ_FRAME_CACHE[_key] = df
            return df
        # ── WHAT IS MISSING, STATED PRECISELY ─────────────────────────────────────────
        # Measured on the owner's frames: basketball carries point_spread_handicap (the LINE)
        # and football carries total_goals_handicap -- but NEITHER carries the spread PRICES
        # (point_spread_home / _away) nor the per-side final scores (final_h_score /
        # final_a_score). Only final_tp and final_result are present.
        # So the market cannot be settled: without per-side finals there is no final margin
        # to compare against the line, and without the spread prices there is nothing to be
        # paid at. This is a SCRAPER gap, not an engine one -- the columns have to be
        # collected before the market can be searched, and inventing either would produce
        # exactly the kind of number this project spent a day removing.
        _have = [c for c in df.columns
                 if any(k in c.lower() for k in ('spread', 'hcap', 'handicap', 'final'))]
        raise ValueError(
            f'{sport}/{market}: cannot settle. Needs the spread PRICES '
            f'(point_spread_home / point_spread_away) and PER-SIDE final scores '
            f'(final_h_score / final_a_score). The frame has {_have}. Add these to the '
            f'scrape and the market becomes searchable — the settlement logic above is '
            f'already in place and waiting for them.')

    fin = next((c for c in ('final_tp_settled', 'final_tp', 'final_tg') if c in df.columns),
               None)
    lin = next((c for c in ('total_points_handicap', 'total_goals_handicap_ffill',
                            'total_goals_handicap', 'open_line') if c in df.columns), None)
    res = next((c for c in ('final_result', 'final_winner') if c in df.columns), None)
    if market == 'under' and fin and lin:
        won = (pd.to_numeric(df[fin], errors='coerce')
               < pd.to_numeric(df[lin], errors='coerce'))
    elif market == 'over' and fin and lin:
        won = (pd.to_numeric(df[fin], errors='coerce')
               > pd.to_numeric(df[lin], errors='coerce'))
    elif market == 'live_lead' and res:
        # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        #  [v76_9] LIVE LEAD — a forecast made DURING the match, never a readback
        # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        #  The owner's actual idea: at the halfway point of set 1, who does the
        #  market think is winning, and does that predict the match? Nothing here
        #  touches set1_winner or any per-match value. The side is decided by the
        #  ODDS ALONE -- each player's current price against that player's own
        #  opening price. Shorter drift = the market moving towards them.
        #
        #      drift_home = home_odds / anchor(home_odds)
        #      back HOME when drift_home < drift_away
        #
        #  Everything is on the screen at the tick: the opening price is the
        #  match's first quote, the current price is now. The selector moves as
        #  the odds move, so it passes the timing detector on its own merits
        #  rather than by exemption. And because the set is still in play the
        #  price is still long -- which is how this market reaches the owner's
        #  1.70 floor when backing a settled set-1 winner never could.
        _g9 = df.groupby('match_id', observed=True)
        _h0 = _g9['home_odds'].transform('first')
        _a0 = _g9['away_odds'].transform('first')
        _dh = pd.to_numeric(df['home_odds'], errors='coerce') / _h0
        _da = pd.to_numeric(df['away_odds'], errors='coerce') / _a0
        _back_home = _dh < _da
        o = pd.Series(np.where(_back_home,
                               pd.to_numeric(df['home_odds'], errors='coerce'),
                               pd.to_numeric(df['away_odds'], errors='coerce')),
                      index=df.index)
        _backed = pd.Series(np.where(_back_home, 'home', 'away'), index=df.index)
        won = (df[res].astype(str).str.strip().str.lower() == _backed)
        # a tick where neither side has moved says nothing -- no bet
        o = o.where(_dh != _da)
        if verbose:
            print(f'[search] {sport}/live_lead: side chosen by odds drift only '
                  f'({int(_back_home.sum()):,} home / {int((~_back_home).sum()):,} '
                  f'away ticks); nothing per-match is read')
    elif market == 'winner' and res:
        side = next((c for c in ('set1_winner', 'set_winner_side') if c in df.columns), None)
        # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        #  [v76_6] SET-1 TIMING SEAL — the tennis market was forward-looking
        # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        #  MEASURED FROM THE OWNER'S OWN BOOK, 20 Aug. The market backs "the SET-1
        #  WINNER to take the match". set1_winner is mapped PER MATCH and
        #  broadcast to every tick, including ticks inside set 1 -- so the engine
        #  was choosing the eventual set-1 winner while set 1 was still being
        #  played, and taking the still-uncertain price. The Phase column showed
        #  it plainly:
        #      SSSS_tenn_01  1,091 bets  1st Set 1,067  (98%)
        #      SSSS_tenn_03  1,143 bets  1st Set   968  (85%)
        #      SSS_tenn_06     535 bets  1st Set   527  (98%)
        #  That is where 80.0% at 2.66 came from: a set-1 winner takes the match
        #  ~80% of the time (the BASE RATE, not an edge) and 2.66 is what an
        #  undecided market pays. Known who wins set 1, the real price is ~1.30.
        #
        #  The forward seal did not catch it: _laz_is_forward refuses final_*,
        #  *_settled and *actual_score*, and 'set1_winner' matches none of those.
        #  The leak was not the COLUMN, it was the TIMING -- a legitimate column
        #  read before the moment it becomes true.
        #
        #  SEALED HERE: a bet may only be struck once set 1 is DECIDED. Judged
        #  from the set-1 scoreline where the frame carries it (6-x with a two
        #  game margin, or a 7 in a tiebreak), and from the phase label otherwise.
        #  Ticks inside set 1 are dropped from the searchable universe entirely,
        #  the same way the feed-death law drops unproven finals.
        # [v76_8] SEALED FROM THE SET COUNT — the same signal that DEFINES the
        # answer. My v76_6 seal tested set-1 GAME scores and the phase label.
        # Both were wrong for this frame:
        #   * the tennis frame's h_score / a_score are SET COUNTS, not games;
        #     the game-score columns were only just added to the scrape
        #   * the phase label is free text and not guaranteed
        # And set1_winner is derived at 32594 from the first INCREMENT in those
        # set counts -- `hinc = h > h.shift()` -- so the moment set 1 is decided
        # is exactly the moment h_score + a_score first reaches 1. Testing
        # anything else was testing a proxy for a signal already in hand.
        #
        # This also rehabilitates the conditions built on it. s1w_pre is the
        # PRE-MATCH price of the set-1 winner: forward-looking before set 1 is
        # over, ordinary history after it. Sealing the timing makes the whole
        # odds-development family -- pm_ratio, anchor, dog_anchor, ten_o,
        # s1w_pre, s1w_sd -- legitimate rather than suspect.
        _decided = None
        if 'h_score' in df.columns and 'a_score' in df.columns:
            _sets = (pd.to_numeric(df['h_score'], errors='coerce').fillna(0)
                     + pd.to_numeric(df['a_score'], errors='coerce').fillna(0))
            _decided = _sets >= 1
        else:
            _ph1 = next((c for c in ('phase', 'ten_phase') if c in df.columns), None)
            if _ph1 is not None:
                _decided = ~df[_ph1].astype(str).str.lower().str.contains(
                    '1st set', na=False)
        if _decided is not None:
            _before = len(df)
            df = df[_decided.fillna(False)]
            if verbose and _before:
                print(f'[search] {sport}/winner: SET-1 SEAL dropped '
                      f'{_before - len(df):,} of {_before:,} ticks struck BEFORE '
                      f'the first set count moved — at those ticks set1_winner '
                      f'was not yet knowable, so the bet knew the answer already')
            _sealed_n = len(df)
        # [v76_7] and now PROVE the selector is causal on the surviving ticks,
        # rather than trusting that the seal above was written correctly.
        if side:
            _laz_assert_selector_causal(df, side, sport, market, verbose=verbose,
                                        sealed=(_decided is not None))
        if side:
            won = (df[res].astype(str).str.strip().str.lower()
                   == df[side].astype(str).str.strip().str.lower())
            o = pd.Series(np.where(df[side].astype(str).str.strip().str.lower() == 'home',
                                   pd.to_numeric(df.get('home_odds'), errors='coerce'),
                                   pd.to_numeric(df.get('away_odds'), errors='coerce')),
                          index=df.index)
    if won is None or o is None:
        raise ValueError(f'{sport}/{market}: cannot settle -- no usable outcome column. '
                         f'frame has {[c for c in df.columns if "win" in c or "result" in c][:6]}')
    df = df.assign(_o=o, _won=won.astype('float64'))
    df['_pnl'] = np.where(df['_won'] > 0, df['_o'] - 1.0, -1.0)
    if market == 'comeback1':
        _gd = (pd.to_numeric(df['h_score'], errors='coerce')
               - pd.to_numeric(df['a_score'], errors='coerce')).abs()
        df = df[_gd == 1]
    ts = next((c for c in ('mstart', 'ts') if c in df.columns), None)
    df = df[df['_o'].notna() & df['_won'].notna() & (df['_o'] >= min_odds)]
    if ts:
        df = df.sort_values(ts)
        q = _laz_match_q(df, ts)
        # IS 35% | FREEZE 30% (sealed) | OOS 35%
        df = df.assign(_win=np.where(q <= 0.35, 'IS',
                                     np.where(q <= 0.65, 'FRZ', 'OOS')))
    else:
        df = df.assign(_win='IS')
    # [v76_15] SET-SPORT BOUNDS as ordinary frame columns, so the search pool,
    # the conditions, the book and the LLM all read them like any other term.
    # Only for sports the RULES bound — a clock sport has no equivalent and
    # inventing one would be a lie.
    if sport in LAZ_SET_SPORT_FORMAT and 'h_score' in df.columns:
        try:
            _need15, _towin15 = LAZ_SET_SPORT_FORMAT[sport]
            _played15, _rmin15, _rmax15 = _laz_set_bounds(df, sport)
            _pts15 = pd.to_numeric(df.get('total_points_scored'), errors='coerce')
            if _pts15 is None or _pts15.isna().all():
                _pts15 = _played15 * _towin15
            df['set_games_left_min'] = _rmin15
            df['set_games_left_max'] = _rmax15
            df['set_total_floor'] = _pts15 + _rmin15 * (_towin15 + 5)
            df['set_total_ceiling'] = _pts15 + _rmax15 * (_towin15 + 10)
        except Exception:
            pass
    df = _laz_feed_complete_filter(sport, market, df, verbose=verbose)
    df = _laz_esports_lockout(sport, market, df, verbose=verbose)
    # [v76_17] REACHABILITY, before a single bet is measured. The owner's catch
    # from validate_book.py, moved from after the book to before the search.
    # [v76_21] Ban the offending COLUMNS before refusing the frame — so even a
    # frame that is allowed through under LAZ_ALLOW_IMPOSSIBLE can never have
    # those features stacked into a strategy.
    try:
        laz_ban_impossible_features(df, sport, verbose=verbose)
    except Exception:
        pass
    laz_assert_frame_reachable(df, sport, market, verbose=verbose)
    if not len(df):
        # [v76_9] SCRAP IT, CLEANLY. The owner's rule: nothing below 1.70 is
        # tradeable, so a market with no surviving bet is not a market -- it is
        # a market he would never place. Returning an empty frame let that fall
        # through into a TypeError several layers down; raising here makes the
        # census record it as NO_DATA and the log say why.
        raise ValueError(
            f'{sport}/{market}: no bet survives at LAZ_MIN_ODDS={min_odds}. '
            + (f'A settled set-1 winner is a SHORT price, so this market cannot '
               f'clear the owner\'s {LAZ_MIN_ODDS_ABSOLUTE} floor — use '
               f'{sport}/live_lead, which backs the side the market is moving '
               f'towards while the set is still open and therefore still long. '
               if market == 'winner' and sport in ('tennis', 'etennis')
               else 'Every candidate price is below the floor. '))
    if (sport in ('tennis', 'etennis') and market == 'winner' and not len(df)
            and verbose):
        # [v76_6] THE INTERACTION WORTH KNOWING ABOUT. Once set 1 is decided the
        # winner is a short price -- often 1.25 to 1.40. LAZ_MIN_ODDS then removes
        # exactly those bets, and what it had been KEEPING were the ticks struck
        # during set 1 at 2.6, which only paid that because the set was still
        # open. The price floor was quietly selecting FOR the leak.
        print(f'[search] {sport}/winner: every tick was removed — the set-1 seal '
              f'drops bets struck before set 1 is decided, and LAZ_MIN_ODDS='
              f'{min_odds} then drops the honest ones because a known set-1 '
              f'winner is a SHORT price. Lower LAZ_MIN_ODDS (try 1.20) to search '
              f'this market causally, or accept that it has no bets above '
              f'{min_odds}.')
    if verbose and len(df):
        print(f'[search] {sport}/{market}: {len(df):,} placeable ticks | '
              f'strike {100*df["_won"].mean():.1f}% | median odds {df["_o"].median():.2f}')
    if use_cache:
        # Evict the oldest first: these frames are hundreds of MB and two is what a 16 GB
        # machine can hold beside the engine.
        while len(_LAZ_FRAME_CACHE) >= _LAZ_FRAME_CACHE_MAX:
            _LAZ_FRAME_CACHE.pop(next(iter(_LAZ_FRAME_CACHE)))
        _LAZ_FRAME_CACHE[_key] = df
    return df


def _laz_search_pool(df, max_feats=40):
    """Threshold candidates from whatever numeric features the frame carries."""
    # `id` and its prefixed forms are MATCH IDENTIFIERS, not market signals. They encode
    # when a match was scraped, so a threshold on one is a date filter wearing a feature's
    # name -- it scores well in a backtest and cannot be evaluated live at all. The tennis
    # search returned two such stacks (65.0% at 3.55 and 70.1% at 2.91) before this.
    skip = {'_o', '_won', '_pnl', '_win', 'h_score', 'a_score', 'home_odds', 'away_odds',
            'final_tp', 'final_tg', 'final_tp_settled', 'ts', 'mstart', 'hour',
            'id', 'match_id', 'ten_id', 'ebb_id', 'efb_id', 'fb_id', 'bb_id',
            'row_id', 'idx', 'index', 'timestamp', 'epoch_ts', 'mend'}
    out = []
    # [v76_33] THE CAMPAIGN STEERS THE POOL. The hp_/ap_/misprice_ columns are
    # appended at the END of the frame by the backfill, so nothing about the
    # enumeration favours them. When LAZ_FOCUS_FAMILY is set, the family's own
    # winning conditions and the player/misprice columns are enumerated FIRST,
    # so every restart that samples early terms samples the campaign's terms.
    # (Noted while here: the max_feats cap below is dead -- len({c for x in
    # out}) is at most 1 -- which is WHY every populated column, player history
    # included, already reaches the pool. Left as-is deliberately: fixing the
    # cap would shrink the pool and silently remove the player vocabulary.)
    import os as _o33p, re as _re33p
    _fam33 = _o33p.environ.get('LAZ_FOCUS_FAMILY', '').strip().lower()
    _cols33 = list(df.columns)
    if _fam33:
        _pri33 = [c for c in _cols33 if _re33p.match(
            r'^(misprice_|hp_|ap_|combined_avg|tg_player|tg_wall|both_|any_'
            r'|cannon_vs_|drift_[ha]$)', str(c))]
        _cols33 = _pri33 + [c for c in _cols33 if c not in set(_pri33)]
        try:
            _frep33, _ = laz_family_study(_fam33, verbose=False)
            for _cc33 in (_frep33.get('winning_conditions') or [])[:12]:
                if isinstance(_cc33, str) and _cc33 not in out:
                    out.append(_cc33)
        except Exception:
            pass
    for c in _cols33:
        if (c in skip or c.startswith('final') or _laz_is_forward(c)
                or not pd.api.types.is_numeric_dtype(df[c])):
            continue
        v = pd.to_numeric(df[c], errors='coerce').dropna()
        if len(v) < len(df) * 0.5 or v.nunique() < 4:
            continue
        # ── [v76_44] TEN INTERVALS FOR EVERY PLAYER COLUMN ──────────────
        #   The owner, 24 Aug: "incorporate all those ap and hp columns and
        #   create good conditions for each with 10 intervals within the
        #   feature." A condition is the COLUMN; its FEATURES are the
        #   thresholds it is tested at. Five quantiles is a coarse net for a
        #   column like hp_avg_goals_scored_l10g whose whole value lies in
        #   WHERE the cut falls; player history therefore gets ten deciles,
        #   everything else keeps five. LAZ_PLAYER_INTERVALS tunes it.
        _isp44 = str(c).startswith(('hp_', 'ap_', 'misprice_', 'combined_avg',
                                    'tg_player', 'h2h_'))
        if _isp44:
            try:
                import os as _oi44
                _ni44 = max(4, int(_oi44.environ.get('LAZ_PLAYER_INTERVALS', '10')))
            except (TypeError, ValueError):
                _ni44 = 10
            _ps44 = [round(0.05 + 0.9 * t / (_ni44 - 1), 4) for t in range(_ni44)]
        else:
            _ps44 = (0.2, 0.35, 0.5, 0.65, 0.8)
        qs = [round(float(v.quantile(p)), 4) for p in _ps44]
        for t in sorted(set(qs)):
            out.append(f'{c} <= {t}')
            out.append(f'{c} >= {t}')
        if len({c for x in out}) >= max_feats:
            break
    return out



# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  ALIAS NORMALISATION — one read, one name                                     [v75_57]
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Several features exist under more than one name. pm_ratio and ten_pm_ratio were made
#  IDENTICAL in v75_8 to fix a divergence; mins, minute, ten_mins and ten_minute are the
#  same elapsed counter; sets and ten_sets are the same tally.
#
#  The search treats them as distinct columns, so it produced stacks like
#      minute <= 40.0 AND mins <= 40.0 AND ten_mins <= 40.0
#  which is ONE condition written three times, and
#      pm_away <= 2.0 AND ten_pm_ratio >= 0.3397 AND ten_pm_home >= 1.24
#  sitting beside the same stack without the third term -- identical bets, two rows.
#  The dedup hashed the condition STRINGS, so it could not see they were the same object,
#  and a book came back with hundreds of rows that were a handful of strategies wearing
#  different spellings.
#
#  Normalising to a canonical name before hashing collapses them. The stack still EVALUATES
#  on whatever column the frame has -- this is only about identity.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

LAZ_ALIAS_CANON = {
    'ten_pm_ratio': 'pm_ratio',
    'ten_minute': 'minute', 'mins': 'minute', 'ten_mins': 'minute',
    'ten_sets': 'sets', 'ten_set_no': 'set_no',
    'ten_pm_home': 'pm_home', 'ten_pm_away': 'pm_away',
    'ten_pm_dog_odds': 'pm_dog_odds', 'ten_dog_ml': 'dog_ml',
    'ten_dog_anchor': 'dog_anchor', 'ten_hour': 'hour',
    'ten_home_odds': 'home_odds', 'ten_away_odds': 'away_odds',
    'ten_o': 'o', 'ten_id': 'id',
    'bb_prog_score': 'prog_score', 'ebb_prog': 'prog', 'efb_prog': 'prog',
    'fb_prog': 'prog', 'ten_prog': 'prog',
    'ebb_qhalf_b': 'qhalf_b', 'bb_q': 'q', 'ebb_q': 'q', 'quarter': 'q',
    # ── [v75_59] Book-evidenced prefixed copies ──────────────────────────────────────
    # The 17 Aug evolve books carried q1_margin and bb_q1_margin as SEPARATE awarded
    # strategies with identical thresholds and the identical definition string ("the
    # first quarter's final scoring margin, signed to the current leader"), and the same
    # for min_since_goal / fb_min_since_goal on football. These are the prefix pass's
    # copies of one read. bb_home_odds / bb_away_odds are the prefixed copies of the
    # price columns (the unprefixed forms are in the search-pool skip set, so only the
    # prefixed spelling ever reaches a stack -- the alias is identity bookkeeping).
    # NOT added: bb_lead. No evidence that a bare `lead` column is the same quantity,
    # and an alias that merges two different features is worse than a duplicate row.
    'bb_q1_margin': 'q1_margin',
    'fb_min_since_goal': 'min_since_goal',
    'bb_home_odds': 'home_odds', 'bb_away_odds': 'away_odds',
}


def laz_canon_stack(conds):
    """Canonical, order-free identity for a stack. Two spellings collapse to one key."""
    out = set()
    for c in conds:
        p = str(c).split()
        if len(p) < 3:
            out.add(str(c))
            continue
        nm = LAZ_ALIAS_CANON.get(p[0], p[0])
        try:
            v = float(p[2])
            out.add(f'{nm} {p[1]} {v:g}')
        except ValueError:
            out.add(f'{nm} {p[1]} {p[2]}')
    return '|'.join(sorted(out))


def _laz_entry_ok(entry):
    """[v75_61] True for entry policies a live system can execute or diagnose.

    'first' and 'tick:K' (K 1..99) are EXECUTABLE: a live system counts qualifying
    ticks as they arrive and strikes on the K-th; a match that never reaches K produces
    no bet, and the backtest mirrors that exactly (nth(K-1) drops smaller groups -- no
    fallback, because a fallback would be a different policy than the live one).
    'best' and 'median' remain DIAGNOSTIC ceilings. 'last' does not exist.

    Note what tick:K is NOT: it is not "strike at X% through the window". The fraction
    of the window is unknowable live -- the denominator is the window's end -- so a
    fractional policy would be the same lookahead entry='last' was banned for. The
    engine's earlier comments claimed otherwise; they were wrong, and the honest
    executable form is the absolute tick count.
    """
    import re as _r
    return entry in ('first', 'best', 'median') or bool(
        _r.fullmatch(r'tick:[1-9][0-9]?', str(entry)))


def laz_search(sport, market=None, n_restarts=800, beam=3, max_depth=None, n_null=3,
               min_is=100, min_oos=100, min_roi=20.0, min_odds=LAZ_MIN_ODDS,
               entry='first',
               verbose=True):
    """Search a sport for stacks clearing IS>=100, OOS>=100, OOS ROI>=20, with a control."""
    import random
    # ── REFUSE LOOKAHEAD ENTRY POLICIES UP FRONT ──────────────────────────────────────
    # Checked here, not inside the per-candidate assess: the branch there only runs when a
    # stack is being scored, so a search that found nothing returned quietly and a caller
    # could believe entry='last' was supported. It is not, and it produced an entire book of
    # fabricated results before this check existed.
    if entry == 'last':
        raise ValueError(
            "entry='last' is lookahead and has been removed. The last qualifying tick "
            "cannot be known until the window closes, so no live system can strike there. "
            "That policy produced a book of 78%-strike stacks at odds 2.99 -- striking last "
            "means striking when the match is nearly decided. Use entry='first'.")
    if not _laz_entry_ok(entry):
        raise ValueError(f"unknown entry policy {entry!r}; use 'first' or 'tick:K' "
                         f"(executable) or 'best' / 'median' for DIAGNOSIS only")
    market = market or list(LAZ_SEARCH_MARKETS.get(sport, {'under': None}))[0]
    df = _laz_search_frame(sport, market, min_odds=min_odds, verbose=verbose)
    # ── [v76_37] OWNER DEPTH RULE — "for massive populations above 1000 in
    # IS/OOS I see nothing wrong with 5 in depth." Adaptive: 5 conditions when
    # the searchable universe holds >= LAZ_BIG_POP (1000) matches, 3 otherwise.
    # Depth stays a cost to justify on thin data and becomes a tool on data
    # that can pay for it.
    if max_depth is None:
        try:
            import os as _od37
            max_depth = 5 if df['match_id'].nunique() >= int(
                _od37.environ.get('LAZ_BIG_POP', '1000')) else 3
        except Exception:
            max_depth = 3
    pool = _laz_search_pool(df)
    # The role axis, appended for every sport. _laz_search_pool derives thresholds from
    # quantiles, which produces sensible cuts for continuous features and nonsense for a
    # 0/1 flag -- so these are stated explicitly rather than inferred.
    _role = [f'{n} {op} {t}' for n, op, ts in LAZ_ROLE_POOL for t in ts
             if n in df.columns]
    if _role:
        pool = pool + _role
        if verbose:
            print(f'[search] +{len(_role)} role thresholds (pre-match favourite axis)')
    # ── FEATURES THE PROPOSER HAS ADDED THIS SESSION ──────────────────────────────────
    # _laz_search_pool derives thresholds from the frame's numeric columns, so a feature
    # admitted mid-run was classified, admitted -- and then never searched, because the pool
    # had already been built without it. Quantile cuts on whatever the proposer added, so
    # the next round can actually stack it.
    _prop_pool = []
    for _pn in list(LAZ_PROPOSED_FEATURES):
        if _pn not in df.columns:
            continue
        _v = pd.to_numeric(df[_pn], errors='coerce').dropna()
        if len(_v) < len(df) * 0.2 or _v.nunique() < 4:
            continue
        for _q in (0.25, 0.5, 0.75):
            _t = round(float(_v.quantile(_q)), 4)
            _prop_pool += [f'{_pn} <= {_t}', f'{_pn} >= {_t}']
    if _prop_pool:
        pool = pool + _prop_pool
        if verbose:
            print(f'[search] +{len(_prop_pool)} threshold(s) from '
                  f'{len(LAZ_PROPOSED_FEATURES)} proposer-added feature(s)')
    if verbose:
        print(f'[search] {len(pool)} threshold candidates | {n_restarts} restarts | '
              f'beam={beam} depth={max_depth} | entry={entry}')

    def assess(conds, pnl=None):
        m = v67_cond_mask(df, conds)
        if m is None:
            return None
        m = np.asarray(m)
        if not m.any():
            return None
        # ── ENTRY TIMING AS A SEARCH AXIS ────────────────────────────────────────────
        # nth(0) takes the FIRST qualifying tick and always has. That is one entry policy
        # among several, and it was never chosen -- it was assumed. The same read struck
        # later can carry a materially different price for the same probability, which is
        # the one dimension that moves win rate and odds independently instead of trading
        # one against the other.
        #   first   the first tick that qualifies                    (the historical policy)
        #   last    the last tick still qualifying -- latest entry, usually longest price
        #   best    the qualifying tick with the LONGEST price. Causal: it picks among
        #           ticks the strategy has already selected, using only prices visible at
        #           each of them. It is a policy a live system can follow by holding while
        #           the condition holds and striking when the price peaks -- NOT a
        #           look-ahead, because no future information decides whether to enter.
        #   median  the middle qualifying tick, as a stability reference
        _grp = df[m].groupby('match_id', sort=False)
        if entry == 'last':
            # ── entry='last' IS LOOKAHEAD ─────────────────────────────────────────────
            # [v75_59] The previous version carried `s = _grp.nth(-1)` as the FIRST branch
            # of this chain, with the refusal below it as an unreachable elif of the same
            # condition -- so the only protection was the check at the top of laz_search,
            # and deleting that one line would have silently restored the lookahead. The
            # implementation is gone now; asking for 'last' raises HERE too, so the
            # refusal is defence in depth rather than a single line.
            # "the last tick where the conditions held" cannot be identified until the
            # window has CLOSED. Live, at any qualifying tick, you do not know whether
            # another one follows -- so this policy requires seeing the future and is not a
            # policy at all.
            # It produced a whole book of fabricated results: 78% strike at odds 2.99 on the
            # set-1-winner market, because striking last means striking when the match is
            # nearly decided. Every awarded row in that book carried entry=last.
            # Kept as an explicit error rather than silently mapped, so a caller that asks
            # for it is told why instead of getting quietly different behaviour.
            raise ValueError(
                "entry='last' is lookahead and has been removed. The last qualifying tick "
                "cannot be known until the window closes, so no live system can strike "
                "there. Use entry='first' (executable), or entry='best' for DIAGNOSIS only "
                "-- best also needs the window's end and is an upper bound, never a "
                "registrable strategy.")
        elif entry == 'best':
            _q = df[m]
            _idx = _q.groupby('match_id', sort=False)['_o'].idxmax()
            s = _q.loc[_idx.dropna()]
        elif entry == 'median':
            _q = df[m].copy()
            _r = _q.groupby('match_id', sort=False).cumcount()
            _n = _q.groupby('match_id', sort=False)['_o'].transform('size')
            s = _q[_r == (_n // 2)]
        elif isinstance(entry, str) and entry.startswith('tick:'):
            # [v75_61] The K-th qualifying tick, no fallback: a match with fewer than K
            # qualifying ticks produces NO bet, live and here alike.
            s = _grp.nth(int(entry.split(':', 1)[1]) - 1)
        else:
            s = _grp.nth(0)
        if len(s) < 40:
            return None
        p = (pnl.loc[s.index] if pnl is not None else s['_pnl'])
        r = _laz_window_metrics(s, pnl=p)   # [v75_124] v13 schema
        r.update(n=len(s), wr=float(100 * (s['_won'] > 0).mean()),
                 odds=float(s['_o'].mean()), roi=float(100 * p.mean()))
        return r

    def run(pnl=None, quiet=False):
        rng = random.Random(42 if pnl is None else 7)
        found = {}
        import time as _tt0
        _hb_t0, _hb_step = _tt0.time(), max(1, n_restarts // 8)
        for i in range(n_restarts):
            # [v75_61] HEARTBEAT. A 7-hour search that prints nothing is
            # indistinguishable from a frozen one — the owner lived that. Every
            # eighth of the way, one line: progress, elapsed, projected total.
            try:
                laz_live_pulse(phase='searching', sport=sport, market=market,
                               entry=entry, restart=i, restarts=n_restarts,
                               pct=100 * i // max(1, n_restarts),
                               best=(max((v.get('wr') or 0)
                                         for v in found.values())
                                     if found else None))
            except Exception:
                pass
            if verbose and i and i % _hb_step == 0:
                _el = _tt0.time() - _hb_t0
                print(f'[search]   ...restart {i}/{n_restarts} '
                      f'({100*i//n_restarts}%) — {_el/60:.1f} min elapsed, '
                      f'~{_el/i*n_restarts/60:.1f} min total at this pace',
                      flush=True)
            seeds = rng.sample(range(len(pool)), min(len(pool), max_depth + 3))
            live = [[]]
            for _ in range(max_depth):
                nxt = []
                for base in live:
                    for si in seeds:
                        cand = base + [pool[si]]
                        # [v75_59] Canonical names, not raw ones. The raw check let alias
                        # spellings of ONE read stack together -- the tennis book carried
                        # `ten_minute <= 24 AND minute <= 24 AND mins <= 40` as a single
                        # strategy, which is one condition written three times. Same-
                        # feature-same-op under canonical identity is refused up front.
                        if len({LAZ_ALIAS_CANON.get(c.split()[0], c.split()[0])
                                + c.split()[1] for c in cand}) != len(cand):
                            continue
                        if not laz_discovery_causal_ok(cand):
                            continue
                        a = assess(cand, pnl)
                        if a is None:
                            continue
                        nxt.append((cand, a))
                if not nxt:
                    break
                nxt.sort(key=lambda t: -(t[1]['OOS_roi'] if t[1]['OOS_roi'] == t[1]['OOS_roi']
                                         else -9e9))
                live = [c for c, _ in nxt[:beam]]
                for c, a in nxt[:beam]:
                    # Canonical key: minute/mins/ten_mins are one read, not three.
                    found[laz_canon_stack(c)] = (c, a)
            if verbose and not quiet and (i + 1) % max(1, n_restarts // 8) == 0:
                print(f'    restart {i+1}/{n_restarts}, {len(found)} stacks')
        return found

    real = run()
    keep = [(c, a) for c, a in real.values()
            if a['IS_n'] >= min_is and a['OOS_n'] >= min_oos and a['OOS_roi'] >= min_roi]
    nulls = []
    for k in range(max(1, n_null)):
        sh = df['_pnl'].sample(frac=1.0, random_state=100 + k).values
        nl = run(pd.Series(sh, index=df.index), quiet=True)
        nulls.append(sum(1 for _c, a in nl.values()
                         if a['IS_n'] >= min_is and a['OOS_n'] >= min_oos
                         and a['OOS_roi'] >= min_roi))
    if verbose:
        print()
        print('=' * 108)
        print(f'  {sport.upper()} / {market.upper()} / entry={entry} — IS>={min_is}, '
              f'OOS>={min_oos}, OOS ROI>={min_roi}')
        print('=' * 108)
        print(f'  found {len(keep)}  ::  permutation null {nulls} from shuffled outcomes')
        if keep and max(nulls) >= len(keep):
            print('  WARNING: noise matched the real count. Nothing below is proven.')
        keep.sort(key=lambda t: -(t[1]['FRZ_roi'] if t[1]['FRZ_roi'] == t[1]['FRZ_roi']
                                  else -9e9))
        for i, (c, a) in enumerate(keep[:20], 1):
            print(f"  {i:2d}  n={a['n']:5d} wr={a['wr']:5.1f}% odds={a['odds']:.2f}  "
                  f"IS {a['IS_n']:4d}/{a['IS_roi']:+6.1f}  OOS {a['OOS_n']:4d}/"
                  f"{a['OOS_roi']:+6.1f}  FRZ {a['FRZ_n']:4d}/{a['FRZ_roi']:+6.1f}")
            print(f"      {' AND '.join(c)}")
        print()
        print('  FREEZE is the middle 30% of matches; no selection step could read it.')
        print('  A stack clearing the bar with a NEGATIVE freeze was fitted -- do not stake.')
    return keep, nulls



# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  laz_evolve — THE CONTINUOUS LOOP                                             [v75_20]
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Two loops running together, because either alone fails a different way.
#
#    EXPLOIT   refine what already works. TNQ05 is ONE condition at 74.3%; can it tighten
#              without losing volume, or enter later for a longer price? A loop that only
#              refines converges on what it already has.
#    EXPLORE   start blank on a market-and-entry combination nobody has searched. A loop
#              that only explores discards what it found.
#
#  The split shifts: when exploit stops producing, weight moves to explore, and back again
#  when a new market opens. Each round is one search, recorded in the ledger whatever it
#  returns -- a null is a fact about the search or about the market, and the ledger
#  distinguishes those two by restart count.
#
#  ENTRY TIMING IS PART OF THE SEARCH SPACE. laz_search took nth(0) for its whole life --
#  one entry policy, never chosen, only assumed. Measured on tennis at 8 restarts:
#      entry=first    4 stacks clear the bar   best 75.3% @ 1.74
#      entry=best    18 stacks clear the bar   best 74.7% @ 1.90
#  Four times the survivors and a longer price for the same read. That is the one axis that
#  moves win rate and odds INDEPENDENTLY rather than trading one against the other, which
#  is exactly what the star ladder demands.
#
#  THE REWARD LADDER, and why the bar comes first
#      60% @ >=2.00  ★        75% @ >=2.00  ★☆       80% @ >=2.00  ★★
#      85% @ >=2.00  ★★★      90% @ >=3.50  ★★★★     90% @ >=5.00  ★★★★★
#  IS >= 100 AND OOS >= 100 is checked BEFORE any star is considered. This project's own
#  books contain 79.3% on 29 bets and 85.7% on 13 -- a ladder that rewarded those would
#  teach the loop that narrow samples are the route to stars, which is the opposite of what
#  is wanted. And no star is awarded until the four gates have passed: a fabricated number
#  must never be rewarded, or the loop learns to fabricate.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── THE LADDER ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Read top-down: the first rung a strategy satisfies is its award. IS >= 100 AND
#  OOS >= 100 is checked BEFORE any of this -- nothing below the bar is ranked at all.
#
#  THE HALF STAR IS A DIFFERENT SHAPE FROM THE REST. Every other rung asks for a high
#  strike rate at a modest price. The half star asks the opposite: ROI >= 60% at a strike
#  rate as low as 15%. That is the long-odds population -- most bets lose and the return
#  comes from a minority of large wins -- and it is a real instrument, not a weaker version
#  of the others. BBT01 sits there: 285 bets, 51.2% at 4.52, OOS +78.7%. So does the
#  football comeback read at 8.0% and +26.7%.
#  It is listed LAST so a strategy that also clears a full rung is awarded the full rung.
# [v75_124] THE EDGE FLOOR, in percentage points over the break-even implied by
# the price actually taken. v13 required +5; the owner's old rule was +10 at
# 2.00 and unreachable at any shorter price. Set LAZ_EDGE_FLOOR to raise it.
import os as _o124
try:
    LAZ_EDGE_FLOOR = float(_o124.environ.get('LAZ_EDGE_FLOOR', '5.0'))
except (TypeError, ValueError):
    LAZ_EDGE_FLOOR = 5.0

# [v75_124] How much of the out-of-sample return the FREEZE window must retain.
# A sign test alone let a +104% OOS / +3.5% FREEZE stack earn a star.
try:
    LAZ_FRZ_RETENTION = float(_o124.environ.get('LAZ_FRZ_RETENTION', '0.25'))
except (TypeError, ValueError):
    LAZ_FRZ_RETENTION = 0.25

