#  v62 SECTION AX — WHAT THE FAILURES TELL US
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Six families were re-optimised and did NOT validate. The pattern is consistent and worth
#  recording, because it says where the remaining edge is not:
#
#    tennis/comeback      CI -2.3%   4/6   drawdown 17.9 -> 13.1  (27% better, still fails)
#    ebasketball/comeback CI -0.0%   4/6   drawdown 16.1
#    basketball/comeback  CI -10.2%  5/6   drawdown 19.6
#    football/inverse     CI -8.0%   4/6
#    basketball/inverse   CI -4.3%   4/6
#    football/misprice    CI +23.8%  6/6   p=0.0680 — the ONLY near miss
#
#  *** THIS CONCLUSION WAS WRONG — SEE SECTION AZ ***
#  I wrote below that the comeback families fail structurally. They do not. Multi-start search with
#  fine threshold refinement validated tennis/comeback (CI -2.3% -> +43.0%, DD 17.9 -> 8.0) and
#  ebasketball/comeback (CI -0.0% -> +41.2%, DD 16.1 -> 7.0). It was the SEARCH that was limited,
#  not the family. Calling a search failure a property of the data is the mistake to avoid.
#
#  EVERY comeback family outside eFootball fails on the CONFIDENCE FLOOR, not on ROI. They show
#  +11% to +23% mean return with a 5th-percentile bootstrap at or below zero. At a 22-29% strike
#  the return lives in a thin tail of winners, and the bad case is not survivable. That is not a
#  fixable-by-search problem; it is the shape of the family.
#
#  football/misprice is different — 6/6 blocks and a +23.8% floor, failing only the permutation
#  test at p=0.0680. It is the one worth revisiting on more data.
#
#  The optimisation DID help even where it did not validate: football/misprice drawdown fell
#  10.6 -> 5.8 while the win rate rose, and tennis/comeback fell 17.9 -> 13.1.
