#  v62 SECTION AY — EXHAUSTIVE LEVELS AND SEED PERMUTATION
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Two admissions about how the search was actually run:
#
#  1. LEVELS WERE NOT EXHAUSTIVE. n_levels=22 takes 22 quantile cuts per column. For a column with
#     400 distinct values that is a coarse grid, and the best cut can sit between two of them.
#     fine_levels() uses EVERY distinct value where cardinality allows and a dense quantile grid
#     otherwise.
#
#  2. EVERY SEARCH STARTED FROM THE SAME PLACE. The forward pass picks the single best condition,
#     then builds on it. That explores ONE region of the space. A condition that is only the third
#     or seventh best on its own may lead to a far better stack once combined — and it was never
#     given the chance. optimise_multiseed() runs an independent search from EACH of the top-K
#     starting conditions and keeps the best final stack, not the best first step.
#
#  This is the difference between a greedy path and a survey of paths.


