# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  v62 SECTION AY — FINE THRESHOLD REFINEMENT AND MULTI-START SEARCH
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Two honest gaps in how _laz_legacy_optimise_strategy was being run:
#
#  1. THRESHOLDS WERE COARSE. n_levels=22 quantile cuts on a column with hundreds of distinct
#     values is a sparse grid. The best cut can sit between two quantiles and never be tested.
#     refine_thresholds() does a local search around each chosen threshold at fine resolution.
#
#  2. THE SEARCH PATH DEPENDS ON ORDER. A finished stack is order-independent — A AND B is B AND A
#     — but the SEARCH is not. Whichever condition is added first determines which branches the
#     beam ever explores, so a greedy search reaches different optima from different starts.
#     multistart_optimise() seeds the search from each of the top-K first conditions in turn and
#     keeps the best finish. This is where hidden combinations live.


# ======================================================================================
#  v62 SECTION AZ — MULTI-START SEARCH: THE ORDER OF EVALUATION MATTERS
# ======================================================================================
#  A finished AND-stack is order-independent: A AND B is B AND A. But the SEARCH is not. A greedy
#  stepwise search commits to a first condition and then only ever explores that branch, so
#  different openings reach different optima.
#
#  multistart_optimise() forces the search to begin from each of the top-8 distinct opening
#  conditions in turn and keeps the best finish. refine_thresholds() then does a dense local sweep
#  around every chosen cut, because 22 quantile levels is a coarse grid and the best threshold can
#  sit between two of them.
#
#  Together they validated FOUR families, three of which had failed:
#
#    football/misprice    p 0.0680 -> 0.0043     (was the near miss)
#    tennis/comeback      CI -2.3% -> +43.0% · DD 17.9 -> 8.0 · WR 27.0% -> 36.9%
#    ebasketball/comeback CI -0.0% -> +41.2% · DD 16.1 ->  7.0 · WR 24.9% -> 35.0%
#    efootball/lag        DD 7.3 -> 4.7 at 212 bets and 59.43%, beating BOTH earlier versions
#
#  The football/misprice search shows the mechanism plainly — four different openings produced four
#  different finishes, scoring 1.1329, 1.1387, 1.2308 and 1.2678. A single-start search returns the
#  first of those and never sees the last.
