# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  v62 SECTION AY — MULTI-START SEARCH AND EXHAUSTIVE THRESHOLDS
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Two limits in how the search was being run, both real:
#
#  1. THRESHOLDS WERE SAMPLED, NOT EXHAUSTED. n_levels=22 takes 22 quantiles per column. The best
#     cut can sit between them. Where a column has few distinct values, every value should be
#     tried; where it has many, a far denser grid should be.
#
#  2. THE SEARCH HAD ONE STARTING POINT. A conjunction is order-independent — A AND B is B AND A —
#     but the GREEDY PATH is not. Whichever condition enters first determines which region the beam
#     explores, so a single run finds one local optimum. Seeding the search from each of the top-K
#     first conditions explores K different regions and keeps the best endpoint.
#
#  This is the difference between "the best stack the greedy path happened to reach" and "the best
#  stack found from several independent starting points".


