# ======================================================================================
#  A new anchor, and the most productive one yet: the side leading by a stated margin at the end
#  of a quarter. Base rates were measured first, then two-way stepwise multi-start was run on each.
#
#  BASE RATES — what a lead is actually worth (basketball, one row per match):
#    end of Q1: 2-4 pts -> 57.6% | 4-6 -> 63.9% | 6-10 -> 76.5% | 10+ -> 88.5%
#    end of Q2: 2-4     -> 61.7% | 4-6 -> 61.1% | 6-10 -> 75.5% | 10+ -> 93.1%
#    end of Q3: 2-4     -> 65.5% | 4-6 -> 73.7% | 6-10 -> 83.5% | 10+ -> 96.7%
#
#  BUT THE PRICE MATTERS MORE THAN THE RATE. Of 12 basketball band/quarter combinations only ONE
#  (Q1 6-10) carries a positive raw edge; the market has fully absorbed the lead by half-time.
#  eBasketball is different — Q3 2-4 pts returns +7.8% BEFORE any condition, on 11,895 matches.
#
#  THE COUNTERINTUITIVE PART: the weakest base produced the best strategy. Basketball Q1 2-4 pts
#  is -6.7% raw and optimises to +39.4%; Q1 6-10 is the only positive base and optimises to only
#  +20.7%. A correctly-priced band has less left to extract. Base rate is the wrong thing to
