# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  lazarus_deployment_spec — INLINED into the engine                        [v68]
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  This was a second file that had to sit beside the engine. If it was missing the book
#  built without Deployment_Spec and Feature_Provenance, silently, and a strategy could
#  show 2,109 OOS bets at +153% ROI while being undeployable because nothing downstream
#  knew what made it fire. One engine, one file, no import to forget.
#
#  The original module docstring, preserved because it records WHY this exists:
#
#    The generator ALREADY captures every strategy's conditions -- evaluate_discovered()
#    puts conditions into its result rows -- but BOTH book writers then DROP that column
#    when rendering. IS_OOS keeps name + metrics only; the Dashboard writer does the same.
#
#    It also fixes a naming defect. Discovered rows are named `family#idx` where idx is
#    the index WITHIN the family, while the registry names the SAME strategy
#    `EFB_<family>_<NN>`. The book therefore lists many strategies twice under two names
#    with different bet counts -- the two rows come from different universe builders.
#    canonical_records() collapses them on laz_strategy_id, a content hash of sport plus
#    the exact conditions, and is therefore immune to naming.
#
#    census_all_strategies() names discovered strategies with a GLOBAL enumerate index,
#    which does NOT match the per-family index the book writers use. Two different `#N`
#    spaces exist in this codebase. Fixed by always going through canonical_records().
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

